window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2019-12-2", "source": "2019年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of having a sense of family responsibility. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "A sense of family responsibility means being willing to care for one's relatives and to shoulder one's proper\nshare of duties at home. In my view, cultivating such an awareness is far from optional; it lies at the very heart\nof a meaningful life.\nFor one thing, family responsibility shapes personal character. When a young person takes the initiative to look\nafter aging parents or help younger siblings with homework, he gradually learns patience, empathy and self-\ndiscipline, qualities that no textbook alone can teach. For another, it holds a family together. My own uncle, for\ninstance, quietly supported his brother's children through college; because of that commitment, the whole\nfamily remains close and warm even today. Moreover, people who honour their duties at home tend to extend\nthe same reliability to society, becoming trustworthy colleagues and citizens.\nIn conclusion, a sense of family responsibility is not a burden but a bond that nurtures individuals, strengthens\nhouseholds and benefits the wider community. We should cherish it and fulfil our duties with a willing heart."}, "translation": {"prompt": "梅花位居中国十大名花之首，源于中国南方，已有三千多年的栽培历史和种植历史。隆冬时节，五颜六色的梅花不畏严寒，迎着风雪傲然绽放。在中国传统文化中，梅花象征着坚强、纯洁、高雅，激励人们不畏艰难、砥砺前行。自古以来，许多诗人和画家从梅花中获取灵感，创作了无数不朽的作品。普通大众也都喜爱梅花，春节期间常用于家庭装饰。南京市已将梅花定为市花，每年举办梅花节，成千上万的人冒着严寒到梅花山踏雪赏梅。", "sample": "The plum blossom ranks first among China’s ten most famous flowers. Originating in southern China, it has a cultivation history of over 3,000 years. In midwinter, the colorful plum blossoms, braving the severe cold, bloom proudly against the wind and snow. In traditional Chinese culture, the plum blossom symbolizes strength, purity and elegance, inspiring people to overcome difficulties and forge ahead. Since ancient times, many poets and painters have drawn inspiration from plum blossoms and created countless immortal works. The general public also love plum blossoms, which are often used for home decoration during the Spring Festival. Nanjing has designated the plum blossom as its city flower and holds a Plum Blossom Festival every year. Thousands of people brave the severe cold and come to Plum Blossom Hill to walk in the snow and enjoy the blossoms."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2019-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The persistent haze over many of our cities is a reminder of the polluted air that we breathe. Over 80% of the world's urban population is breathing air that fails to meet World Health Organisation guidelines, and an estimated 4.5 million people died  __26__  from outdoor air pollution in 2015.\nGlobally, urban populations are expected to double in the next 40 years, and an extra 2 billion people will need new places to live, as well as services and ways to move around their cities. What is more important, the decisions that we make now about the design of our cities will  __27__  the everyday lives and health of the coming generations. So what would a smog-free, or at least low-pollution, city be like?\nTraffic has become  __28__  with air pollution, and many countries intend to ban the sale of petrol and diesel cars in the next two decades. But simply  __29__  to electric cars will not mean pollution-free cities. The level of emissions they cause will depend on how the electricity to run them is  __30__  , while brakes, tyres and roads all create tiny airborne  __31__  as they wear out.\nAcross the developed world, car use is in decline as more people move to city centres, while young people especially are  __32__  for other means of travel. Researchers are already asking if motor vehicle use has reached its  __33__  and will decline, but transport planners have yet to catch up with this  __34__  , instead of laying new roads to tackle traffic jams. As users of London's orbital M25 motorway will know, new roads rapidly fill with more traffic. In the US, studies have shown that doubling the size of a road can  __35__  double the traffic, taking us back to the starting point.", "matchIntro": "How Much Protein Do You Really Need?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The marketing is enticing: Get stronger muscles and healthier bodies with minimal effort by adding protein powder to your morning shake or juice drink. Or grab a protein bar at lunch or for a quick snack. Today, you can find protein supplements everywhere—online or at the pharmacy, grocery store or health food store. They come in powders, pills and bars. With more than $12 billion in sales this year, the industry is booming and, according to the market research company, Grand View Research, is on track to sell billions more by 2025. But do we really need all this supplemental protein? It depends. There are pros, cons and some other things to consider."}, {"label": "B", "text": "For starters, protein is critical for every cell in our body. It helps build nails, hair, bones, and muscles. It can also help you feel fuller longer than eating foods without protein. And, unlike nutrients that are found only in a few foods, protein is present in all foods. \"The typical American diet is a lot higher in protein than a lot of us think,\" says registered dietitian Angela Pipitone. \"It's in foods many of us expect, such as beef, chicken and other types of meat and dairy. But it's also in foods that may not come immediately to mind like vegetables, fruit, beans and grains.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "The U.S. government's recommended daily allowance (RDA) for the average adult is 50 to 60 grams of protein a day. This may sound like a lot, but Pipitone says: \"We get bits of protein here and there and that really adds up throughout the day.\" Take, for example, breakfast. If you ate two eggs topped with a little bit of cheese and an orange on the side, you already have 22 grams of protein. Each egg gives you 7 grams, the cheese gives you around 6 grams and the orange—about 2 grams. Add a lunch of chicken, rice and broccoli(西兰花), and you are already over the recommended 50 grams. \"You can get enough protein and meet the RDA before you even get to dinner,\" says Pipitone."}, {"label": "D", "text": "So if it's so easy to get your protein in food, why add more in the form of powders, snack bars or a boost at your local juice bar? No need to, says Pipitone because, in fact, most of us already get enough protein in our diet. \"Whole foods are always the best option rather than adding supplements,\" she says, noting the FDA does not regulate supplements as stringently as foods or drugs, so there could be less protein, more sugar and some additives you wouldn't expect, such as caffeine(咖啡因)."}, {"label": "E", "text": "If you are considering a supplement, read the list of ingredients, she says, although this is not always reliable. \"I've seen very expensive protein supplements that claim to be high quality but they might not really be beneficial for the average healthy adult,\" she says. \"It could just be a waste of money.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "But there are certain situations that do warrant extra protein. \"Anytime you're repairing or building muscle,\" Pipitone says, such as if you're an extreme endurance athlete, training for a marathon, or you're a body builder. If you're moderately exercising for 150 minutes a week, as the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends, or less than that, you're probably not an extreme athlete. Extreme athletes expend lots of energy breaking down and repairing and building muscles. Protein can give them the edge they need to speed along that process."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Vegans can benefit from protein supplements since they do not eat animal-based protein sources like meat, dairy or eggs. And, for someone always on-the-go who may not have time for a meal, a protein snack bar can be a good option for occasional meal replacement. Also, individuals recovering from surgery or an injury can also benefit from extra protein. So, too, can older people. At around age 60, \"muscles really start to break down,\" says Kathryn Starr, an aging researcher, \"and because of that, the protein needs of an older adult actually increase.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "In fact, along with her colleague Connie Bales, Starr recently conducted a small study that found that adding extra protein foods to the diet of obese older individuals who were trying to lose weight strengthened their muscles. Participants in the study were separated into two groups—one group was asked to eat 30 grams of protein per meal in the form of whole foods. That meant they were eating 90 grams of protein a day. The other group—the control group—was put on a typical low-calorie diet with about 50 to 60 grams of protein a day. After six months, researchers found the high protein group had significantly improved their muscle function—almost twice as much as the control group. \"They were able to walk faster, had improved balance, and were also able to get up out of a chair faster than the control group,\" Starr says. All 67 participants were over 60 years of age, and both groups lost about the same amount of weight."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Starr is now looking into whether high-protein diets also improve the quality of the muscle itself in seniors. She's using CT scans to measure muscle size and fat, and comparing seniors on a high-protein diet to those on regular diets. She says her findings should be available in a couple of months."}, {"label": "J", "text": "In the meantime, 70-year-old Corliss Keith, who was in the high protein group in Starr's latest study, says she feels a big difference. \"I feel excellent,\" she says, \"I feel like I have a different body, I have more energy, I'm stronger.\" She says she is able to take Zumba exercise classes three times a week, work out on the treadmill(跑步机), and take long, brisk walks. Keith also lost more than 15 pounds. \"I'm a fashionable person, so now I'm back in my three-inch heels,\" she says."}, {"label": "K", "text": "As people age, Starr says muscle strength is key to helping them stay strong and continue living on their own in their own home. \"I feel very much alive now,\" says Keith. \"I feel like I could stay by myself until I'm 100.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "But can people overdo protein? Pipitone says you do have to be careful. Other researchers say too much protein can cause cramps(痉挛), headaches, and fatigue. Dehydration(脱水) is also a risk when you eat too much protein. Pipitone says if you increase protein, you also have to increase your fluid intake. \"I always tell people to make sure they're drinking enough fluids,\" which for the average person is 60 to 70 ounces a day, which translates into eight 8-ounce glasses of water or liquid per day."}, {"label": "M", "text": "There have been some indications that extra protein makes the kidneys work harder, which could be problematic for individuals with a history of kidney disease and for them, the supplements may increase the risk of kidney stones, she says."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Bottom Line: if you think you need more protein in your diet, consider these questions: Are you an extreme athlete; are you recovering from injury or surgery; or are you 60 years or older? If so, adding high protein foods like eggs and meat products to your diet can be beneficial. And, if you're not sure, it is always a good idea to check with your primary care provider."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Last year, a child was born at a hospital in the UK with her heart outside her body. Few babies survive this rare condition, and those who do must endure numerous operations and are likely to have complex needs. When her mother was interviewed, three weeks after her daughter's birth, she was asked if she was prepared for what might be a daunting(令人生畏的) task caring for her. She answered without hesitation that, as far as she was concerned, this would be a \"privilege\".\nRarely has there been a better example of the power of attitude, one of our most powerful psychological tools. Our attitudes allow us to turn mistakes into opportunities, and loss into the chance for new beginnings. An attitude is a settled way of thinking, feeling and/or behaving towards particular objects, people, events or ideologies. We use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us. You weren't born with attitudes; rather they are all learned, and this happens in a number of ways.\nThe most powerful influences occur during early childhood and include both what happened to you directly, and what those around you did and said in your presence. As you acquire a distinctive identity your attitudes are further refined by the behavior of those with whom you identify—your family, those of your gender and culture, and the people you admire, even though you may not know them personally. Friendships and other important relationships become increasingly important, particularly during adolescence. About that same time and throughout adulthood, the information you receive, especially when ideas are repeated in association with goals and achievements you find attractive, also refines your attitudes.\nMany people assume that our attitudes are internally consistent, that is, the way you think and feel about someone or something predicts your behavior towards them. However, many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't necessarily predict behavior. In general, your attitudes will be internally consistent only when the behavior is easy, and when those around you hold similar beliefs. That's why, for example, many say they believe in the benefits of recycling or exercise, but don't behave in line with their views, because it takes awareness, effort and courage to go beyond merely stating that you believe something is a good idea.\nOne of the most effective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to. Take some time to reflect on your attitudes, to think about what you believe and why. Is there anything you consider a burden rather than a privilege? If so, start behaving—right now—as if the latter is the case.", "47": "Last year, a child was born at a hospital in the UK with her heart outside her body. Few babies survive this rare condition, and those who do must endure numerous operations and are likely to have complex needs. When her mother was interviewed, three weeks after her daughter's birth, she was asked if she was prepared for what might be a daunting(令人生畏的) task caring for her. She answered without hesitation that, as far as she was concerned, this would be a \"privilege\".\nRarely has there been a better example of the power of attitude, one of our most powerful psychological tools. Our attitudes allow us to turn mistakes into opportunities, and loss into the chance for new beginnings. An attitude is a settled way of thinking, feeling and/or behaving towards particular objects, people, events or ideologies. We use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us. You weren't born with attitudes; rather they are all learned, and this happens in a number of ways.\nThe most powerful influences occur during early childhood and include both what happened to you directly, and what those around you did and said in your presence. As you acquire a distinctive identity your attitudes are further refined by the behavior of those with whom you identify—your family, those of your gender and culture, and the people you admire, even though you may not know them personally. Friendships and other important relationships become increasingly important, particularly during adolescence. About that same time and throughout adulthood, the information you receive, especially when ideas are repeated in association with goals and achievements you find attractive, also refines your attitudes.\nMany people assume that our attitudes are internally consistent, that is, the way you think and feel about someone or something predicts your behavior towards them. However, many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't necessarily predict behavior. In general, your attitudes will be internally consistent only when the behavior is easy, and when those around you hold similar beliefs. That's why, for example, many say they believe in the benefits of recycling or exercise, but don't behave in line with their views, because it takes awareness, effort and courage to go beyond merely stating that you believe something is a good idea.\nOne of the most effective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to. Take some time to reflect on your attitudes, to think about what you believe and why. Is there anything you consider a burden rather than a privilege? If so, start behaving—right now—as if the latter is the case.", "48": "Last year, a child was born at a hospital in the UK with her heart outside her body. Few babies survive this rare condition, and those who do must endure numerous operations and are likely to have complex needs. When her mother was interviewed, three weeks after her daughter's birth, she was asked if she was prepared for what might be a daunting(令人生畏的) task caring for her. She answered without hesitation that, as far as she was concerned, this would be a \"privilege\".\nRarely has there been a better example of the power of attitude, one of our most powerful psychological tools. Our attitudes allow us to turn mistakes into opportunities, and loss into the chance for new beginnings. An attitude is a settled way of thinking, feeling and/or behaving towards particular objects, people, events or ideologies. We use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us. You weren't born with attitudes; rather they are all learned, and this happens in a number of ways.\nThe most powerful influences occur during early childhood and include both what happened to you directly, and what those around you did and said in your presence. As you acquire a distinctive identity your attitudes are further refined by the behavior of those with whom you identify—your family, those of your gender and culture, and the people you admire, even though you may not know them personally. Friendships and other important relationships become increasingly important, particularly during adolescence. About that same time and throughout adulthood, the information you receive, especially when ideas are repeated in association with goals and achievements you find attractive, also refines your attitudes.\nMany people assume that our attitudes are internally consistent, that is, the way you think and feel about someone or something predicts your behavior towards them. However, many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't necessarily predict behavior. In general, your attitudes will be internally consistent only when the behavior is easy, and when those around you hold similar beliefs. That's why, for example, many say they believe in the benefits of recycling or exercise, but don't behave in line with their views, because it takes awareness, effort and courage to go beyond merely stating that you believe something is a good idea.\nOne of the most effective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to. Take some time to reflect on your attitudes, to think about what you believe and why. Is there anything you consider a burden rather than a privilege? If so, start behaving—right now—as if the latter is the case.", "49": "Last year, a child was born at a hospital in the UK with her heart outside her body. Few babies survive this rare condition, and those who do must endure numerous operations and are likely to have complex needs. When her mother was interviewed, three weeks after her daughter's birth, she was asked if she was prepared for what might be a daunting(令人生畏的) task caring for her. She answered without hesitation that, as far as she was concerned, this would be a \"privilege\".\nRarely has there been a better example of the power of attitude, one of our most powerful psychological tools. Our attitudes allow us to turn mistakes into opportunities, and loss into the chance for new beginnings. An attitude is a settled way of thinking, feeling and/or behaving towards particular objects, people, events or ideologies. We use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us. You weren't born with attitudes; rather they are all learned, and this happens in a number of ways.\nThe most powerful influences occur during early childhood and include both what happened to you directly, and what those around you did and said in your presence. As you acquire a distinctive identity your attitudes are further refined by the behavior of those with whom you identify—your family, those of your gender and culture, and the people you admire, even though you may not know them personally. Friendships and other important relationships become increasingly important, particularly during adolescence. About that same time and throughout adulthood, the information you receive, especially when ideas are repeated in association with goals and achievements you find attractive, also refines your attitudes.\nMany people assume that our attitudes are internally consistent, that is, the way you think and feel about someone or something predicts your behavior towards them. However, many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't necessarily predict behavior. In general, your attitudes will be internally consistent only when the behavior is easy, and when those around you hold similar beliefs. That's why, for example, many say they believe in the benefits of recycling or exercise, but don't behave in line with their views, because it takes awareness, effort and courage to go beyond merely stating that you believe something is a good idea.\nOne of the most effective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to. Take some time to reflect on your attitudes, to think about what you believe and why. Is there anything you consider a burden rather than a privilege? If so, start behaving—right now—as if the latter is the case.", "50": "Last year, a child was born at a hospital in the UK with her heart outside her body. Few babies survive this rare condition, and those who do must endure numerous operations and are likely to have complex needs. When her mother was interviewed, three weeks after her daughter's birth, she was asked if she was prepared for what might be a daunting(令人生畏的) task caring for her. She answered without hesitation that, as far as she was concerned, this would be a \"privilege\".\nRarely has there been a better example of the power of attitude, one of our most powerful psychological tools. Our attitudes allow us to turn mistakes into opportunities, and loss into the chance for new beginnings. An attitude is a settled way of thinking, feeling and/or behaving towards particular objects, people, events or ideologies. We use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us. You weren't born with attitudes; rather they are all learned, and this happens in a number of ways.\nThe most powerful influences occur during early childhood and include both what happened to you directly, and what those around you did and said in your presence. As you acquire a distinctive identity your attitudes are further refined by the behavior of those with whom you identify—your family, those of your gender and culture, and the people you admire, even though you may not know them personally. Friendships and other important relationships become increasingly important, particularly during adolescence. About that same time and throughout adulthood, the information you receive, especially when ideas are repeated in association with goals and achievements you find attractive, also refines your attitudes.\nMany people assume that our attitudes are internally consistent, that is, the way you think and feel about someone or something predicts your behavior towards them. However, many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't necessarily predict behavior. In general, your attitudes will be internally consistent only when the behavior is easy, and when those around you hold similar beliefs. That's why, for example, many say they believe in the benefits of recycling or exercise, but don't behave in line with their views, because it takes awareness, effort and courage to go beyond merely stating that you believe something is a good idea.\nOne of the most effective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to. Take some time to reflect on your attitudes, to think about what you believe and why. Is there anything you consider a burden rather than a privilege? If so, start behaving—right now—as if the latter is the case.", "51": "Industrial fishing for krill(磷虾) in the unspoilt waters around Antarctica is threatening the future of one of the world's last great wildernesses, according to a new report.\nThe study by Greenpeace analysed the movements of krill fishing vessels in the region and found they were increasingly operating \"in the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and whale-feeding grounds\". It also highlights incidents of fishing boats being involved in groundings, oil spills and accidents, which posed a serious threat to the Antarctic ecosystem.\nThe report, published on Tuesday, comes amid growing concern about the impact of fishing and climate change on the Antarctic. A global campaign has been launched to create a network of ocean sanctuaries to protect the seas in the region and Greenpeace is calling for an immediate halt to fishing in areas being considered for sanctuary status.\nFrida Bengtsson from Greenpeace's Protect the Antarctic campaign said: \"If the krill industry wants to show it's a responsible player, then it should be voluntarily getting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary, and should instead be backing the protection of these huge tracts of the Antarctic.\"\nA global campaign has been launched to turn a huge tract of Antarctic seas into ocean sanctuaries protecting wildlife and banning not just krill fishing, but all fishing. One was created in the Ross Sea in 2016, another reserve is being proposed in a vast area of the Weddell Sea, and a third sanctuary is under consideration in the area west of the Antarctic Peninsula—a key krill fishing area.\nThe Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR) manages the seas around Antarctica. It will decide on the Weddell Sea sanctuary proposal at a conference in Australia in October, although a decision on the peninsula sanctuary is not expected until later.\nKeith Reid, a science manager at CCAMLR, said that the organisation sought \"a balance between protection, conservation and sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean.\" He said although more fishing was taking place nearer penguin colonies it was often happening later in the season when these colonies were empty.\n\"The creation of a system of marine protected areas is a key part of ongoing scientific and policy discussions in CCAMLR,\" he added. \"Our long-term operation in the region depends on a healthy and thriving Antarctic marine ecosystem, which is why we have always had an open dialogue with the environmental non-governmental organisations. We strongly intend to continue this dialogue, including talks with Greenpeace, to discuss improvements based on the latest scientific data. We are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas, but we hope to contribute positively with our knowledge and experience.\"", "52": "Industrial fishing for krill(磷虾) in the unspoilt waters around Antarctica is threatening the future of one of the world's last great wildernesses, according to a new report.\nThe study by Greenpeace analysed the movements of krill fishing vessels in the region and found they were increasingly operating \"in the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and whale-feeding grounds\". It also highlights incidents of fishing boats being involved in groundings, oil spills and accidents, which posed a serious threat to the Antarctic ecosystem.\nThe report, published on Tuesday, comes amid growing concern about the impact of fishing and climate change on the Antarctic. A global campaign has been launched to create a network of ocean sanctuaries to protect the seas in the region and Greenpeace is calling for an immediate halt to fishing in areas being considered for sanctuary status.\nFrida Bengtsson from Greenpeace's Protect the Antarctic campaign said: \"If the krill industry wants to show it's a responsible player, then it should be voluntarily getting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary, and should instead be backing the protection of these huge tracts of the Antarctic.\"\nA global campaign has been launched to turn a huge tract of Antarctic seas into ocean sanctuaries protecting wildlife and banning not just krill fishing, but all fishing. One was created in the Ross Sea in 2016, another reserve is being proposed in a vast area of the Weddell Sea, and a third sanctuary is under consideration in the area west of the Antarctic Peninsula—a key krill fishing area.\nThe Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR) manages the seas around Antarctica. It will decide on the Weddell Sea sanctuary proposal at a conference in Australia in October, although a decision on the peninsula sanctuary is not expected until later.\nKeith Reid, a science manager at CCAMLR, said that the organisation sought \"a balance between protection, conservation and sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean.\" He said although more fishing was taking place nearer penguin colonies it was often happening later in the season when these colonies were empty.\n\"The creation of a system of marine protected areas is a key part of ongoing scientific and policy discussions in CCAMLR,\" he added. \"Our long-term operation in the region depends on a healthy and thriving Antarctic marine ecosystem, which is why we have always had an open dialogue with the environmental non-governmental organisations. We strongly intend to continue this dialogue, including talks with Greenpeace, to discuss improvements based on the latest scientific data. We are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas, but we hope to contribute positively with our knowledge and experience.\"", "53": "Industrial fishing for krill(磷虾) in the unspoilt waters around Antarctica is threatening the future of one of the world's last great wildernesses, according to a new report.\nThe study by Greenpeace analysed the movements of krill fishing vessels in the region and found they were increasingly operating \"in the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and whale-feeding grounds\". It also highlights incidents of fishing boats being involved in groundings, oil spills and accidents, which posed a serious threat to the Antarctic ecosystem.\nThe report, published on Tuesday, comes amid growing concern about the impact of fishing and climate change on the Antarctic. A global campaign has been launched to create a network of ocean sanctuaries to protect the seas in the region and Greenpeace is calling for an immediate halt to fishing in areas being considered for sanctuary status.\nFrida Bengtsson from Greenpeace's Protect the Antarctic campaign said: \"If the krill industry wants to show it's a responsible player, then it should be voluntarily getting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary, and should instead be backing the protection of these huge tracts of the Antarctic.\"\nA global campaign has been launched to turn a huge tract of Antarctic seas into ocean sanctuaries protecting wildlife and banning not just krill fishing, but all fishing. One was created in the Ross Sea in 2016, another reserve is being proposed in a vast area of the Weddell Sea, and a third sanctuary is under consideration in the area west of the Antarctic Peninsula—a key krill fishing area.\nThe Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR) manages the seas around Antarctica. It will decide on the Weddell Sea sanctuary proposal at a conference in Australia in October, although a decision on the peninsula sanctuary is not expected until later.\nKeith Reid, a science manager at CCAMLR, said that the organisation sought \"a balance between protection, conservation and sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean.\" He said although more fishing was taking place nearer penguin colonies it was often happening later in the season when these colonies were empty.\n\"The creation of a system of marine protected areas is a key part of ongoing scientific and policy discussions in CCAMLR,\" he added. \"Our long-term operation in the region depends on a healthy and thriving Antarctic marine ecosystem, which is why we have always had an open dialogue with the environmental non-governmental organisations. We strongly intend to continue this dialogue, including talks with Greenpeace, to discuss improvements based on the latest scientific data. We are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas, but we hope to contribute positively with our knowledge and experience.\"", "54": "Industrial fishing for krill(磷虾) in the unspoilt waters around Antarctica is threatening the future of one of the world's last great wildernesses, according to a new report.\nThe study by Greenpeace analysed the movements of krill fishing vessels in the region and found they were increasingly operating \"in the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and whale-feeding grounds\". It also highlights incidents of fishing boats being involved in groundings, oil spills and accidents, which posed a serious threat to the Antarctic ecosystem.\nThe report, published on Tuesday, comes amid growing concern about the impact of fishing and climate change on the Antarctic. A global campaign has been launched to create a network of ocean sanctuaries to protect the seas in the region and Greenpeace is calling for an immediate halt to fishing in areas being considered for sanctuary status.\nFrida Bengtsson from Greenpeace's Protect the Antarctic campaign said: \"If the krill industry wants to show it's a responsible player, then it should be voluntarily getting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary, and should instead be backing the protection of these huge tracts of the Antarctic.\"\nA global campaign has been launched to turn a huge tract of Antarctic seas into ocean sanctuaries protecting wildlife and banning not just krill fishing, but all fishing. One was created in the Ross Sea in 2016, another reserve is being proposed in a vast area of the Weddell Sea, and a third sanctuary is under consideration in the area west of the Antarctic Peninsula—a key krill fishing area.\nThe Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR) manages the seas around Antarctica. It will decide on the Weddell Sea sanctuary proposal at a conference in Australia in October, although a decision on the peninsula sanctuary is not expected until later.\nKeith Reid, a science manager at CCAMLR, said that the organisation sought \"a balance between protection, conservation and sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean.\" He said although more fishing was taking place nearer penguin colonies it was often happening later in the season when these colonies were empty.\n\"The creation of a system of marine protected areas is a key part of ongoing scientific and policy discussions in CCAMLR,\" he added. \"Our long-term operation in the region depends on a healthy and thriving Antarctic marine ecosystem, which is why we have always had an open dialogue with the environmental non-governmental organisations. We strongly intend to continue this dialogue, including talks with Greenpeace, to discuss improvements based on the latest scientific data. We are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas, but we hope to contribute positively with our knowledge and experience.\"", "55": "Industrial fishing for krill(磷虾) in the unspoilt waters around Antarctica is threatening the future of one of the world's last great wildernesses, according to a new report.\nThe study by Greenpeace analysed the movements of krill fishing vessels in the region and found they were increasingly operating \"in the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and whale-feeding grounds\". It also highlights incidents of fishing boats being involved in groundings, oil spills and accidents, which posed a serious threat to the Antarctic ecosystem.\nThe report, published on Tuesday, comes amid growing concern about the impact of fishing and climate change on the Antarctic. A global campaign has been launched to create a network of ocean sanctuaries to protect the seas in the region and Greenpeace is calling for an immediate halt to fishing in areas being considered for sanctuary status.\nFrida Bengtsson from Greenpeace's Protect the Antarctic campaign said: \"If the krill industry wants to show it's a responsible player, then it should be voluntarily getting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary, and should instead be backing the protection of these huge tracts of the Antarctic.\"\nA global campaign has been launched to turn a huge tract of Antarctic seas into ocean sanctuaries protecting wildlife and banning not just krill fishing, but all fishing. One was created in the Ross Sea in 2016, another reserve is being proposed in a vast area of the Weddell Sea, and a third sanctuary is under consideration in the area west of the Antarctic Peninsula—a key krill fishing area.\nThe Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR) manages the seas around Antarctica. It will decide on the Weddell Sea sanctuary proposal at a conference in Australia in October, although a decision on the peninsula sanctuary is not expected until later.\nKeith Reid, a science manager at CCAMLR, said that the organisation sought \"a balance between protection, conservation and sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean.\" He said although more fishing was taking place nearer penguin colonies it was often happening later in the season when these colonies were empty.\n\"The creation of a system of marine protected areas is a key part of ongoing scientific and policy discussions in CCAMLR,\" he added. \"Our long-term operation in the region depends on a healthy and thriving Antarctic marine ecosystem, which is why we have always had an open dialogue with the environmental non-governmental organisations. We strongly intend to continue this dialogue, including talks with Greenpeace, to discuss improvements based on the latest scientific data. We are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas, but we hope to contribute positively with our knowledge and experience.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2019-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman's store?", "options": ["A) It focuses exclusively on jazz.", "B) It sponsors major jazz concerts.", "C) It has several branches in London.", "D) It displays albums by new music talents."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It focuses exclusively on jazz.\n【定位】We're the only record store in London dedicated exclusively to jazz.\n我们是伦敦唯一一家专门只做爵士乐的唱片店。\n【信号】绝对范围词 only/exclusively 即落点。答案句里连用 the only record store 和 dedicated\nexclusively to jazz，only 与 exclusively 这种绝对范围词把店铺最核心的属性\"只做爵士\"点\n了出来，正是承载答案的信号词。\n【替换】dedicated exclusively to jazz ↔ focuses exclusively on jazz\ndedicated to=focuses on同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说店里有售票处 you can buy tickets to all the major jazz concerts，是\"卖音乐会的\n票\"，B 项却说店\"赞助 sponsors\"大型音乐会，盗用了 major jazz concerts 这串原词，却\n把\"卖票\"偷换成\"赞助\"，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：you can buy tickets to all the major jazz concerts in the city\nC) 与原文相反\n录音强调它是 the only record store in London（伦敦唯一一家），C 项却说它\"在伦敦有\n好几家分店 several branches\"，与原文正好相反；branches 在录音里根本没提。\n借⽤原⽂：the only record store in London dedicated exclusively to jazz\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说店里自有录音棚 we produce albums for up-and-coming artists、fostering new\nmusic talent，是\"制作\"新人专辑、\"培养\"新人；D 项却说\"陈列 displays\"新人专辑，盗用\n了 albums 和 new music talent 的原词，把\"制作 / 培养\"偷换成\"展示\"。\n借⽤原⽂：we produce albums for up-and-coming artists. We are committed to fostering new\nmusic talent", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say about jazz music?", "options": ["A) It originated with cowboys.", "B) Its market has now shrunk.", "C) Its listeners are mostly young people.", "D) It remains as widespread as hip hop music."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Its market has now shrunk.\n【定位】I guess there's not much of a jazz scene anymore, not like they used to be.\n我想现在爵士乐的氛围已经不像从前那样兴盛了。\n【信号】今昔对比 anymore/used to be。这句是男士（M）说的，not much of a jazz scene\nanymore 配 not like they used to be 构成\"如今不如从前\"的今昔对比，正是承载\"市场萎\n缩\"这一答案的信号；同时要认准这是 M 的话而非 W。\n【替换】there's not much of a jazz scene anymore, not like they used to be ↔ Its market has now shrunk\n氛围不如从前=市场已缩小\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项说爵士\"起源于牛仔 originated with cowboys\"，整段对话没有任何关于爵士起源的内\n容，属凭空捏造，听感上\"牛仔\"还容易和西部 / 乡村乐混淆。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说如今 there are fewer listeners of jazz（爵士听众变少），并未说听众\"大多是年轻\n人\"；反而说年轻人爱听的是 pop、rock、hip hop，C 项与原文取向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：there are fewer listeners of jazz\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 Hip hop music from America is also getting more and more popular，是说嘻\n哈越来越火、反衬爵士遇冷；D 项却说爵士\"和嘻哈一样普及 as widespread as hip hop\"，\n盗用 hip hop 原词，捏造出二者同样流行的关系。\n借⽤原⽂：Hip hop music from America is also getting more and more popular", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman say about jazz?", "options": ["A) Its definition is varied and complicated.", "B) It is still going through experimentation.", "C) It is frequently accompanied by singing.", "D) Its style has remained largely unchanged"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Its definition is varied and complicated.\n【定位】Well, interestingly enough, there's no agreed-upon definition of jazz. Indeed, there are many\ndifferent styles of jazz.\n嗯，有意思的是，爵士并没有一个公认的定义。确实，爵士有许多不同的风格。\n【信号】被问者直接应答定义问题。男士前一句问 how would you define jazz?，女士的回答正是\n答案句：there's no agreed-upon definition 加 there are many different styles，被问者\n对\"定义\"问题的直接应答就是听力定位的落点。\n【替换】there's no agreed-upon definition of jazz ↔ Its definition is varied and complicated\n无公认定义=定义多样复杂\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说 Some contain live experimentation, but not always——只有\"部分\"爵士含现场\n即兴、并非总是；B 项却说爵士\"仍在经历试验 still going through experimentation\"，盗\n用 experimentation 原词，把\"部分、不总是\"夸成\"整体仍在试验\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Some contain live experimentation, but not always\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说 Some have singing, but most don't——有歌唱的只是少数、大多没有；C 项却说\n爵士\"常常伴有歌唱 frequently accompanied by singing\"，盗用 singing 原词，把\"most\ndon't（多数没有）\"反着说成\"frequently（经常有）\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Some have singing, but most don't\nD) 与原文相反\n录音反复强调爵士 many different styles of jazz（风格多样），D 项却说其风格\"基本未变\nremained largely unchanged\"，与原文取向正好相反，是第 3 题预读时就标出的对立陷阱\n项。\n借⽤原⽂：there are many different styles of jazz", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What should you do to appreciate different styles of jazz according to the woman?", "options": ["A) Learn to play them.", "B) Take music lessons.", "C) Listen to them yourself.", "D) Consult jazz musicians."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Listen to them yourself.\n【定位】Honestly, the only way to know what jazz is, is to listen to it yourself.\n老实说，了解爵士到底是什么的唯一办法，就是你自己去听。\n【信号】唯一办法句 the only way…is to。答案句用 the only way to know what jazz is, is to\nlisten to it yourself，the only way…is to 是最强的建议 / 方法信号，把\"唯一办法\"直接点\n给你，正是承载答案的落点句。\n【替换】the only way to know what jazz is, is to listen to it yourself ↔ Listen to them yourself.\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\n女士只说\"自己去听\"，从未建议\"学着去演奏 Learn to play them\"；演奏与本题\"欣赏\"无\n关，原文无此落点。\nB) 未提及\nB 项\"去上音乐课 Take music lessons\"听起来很合理，但女士给的唯一办法是\"亲自听\"，并\n没有让人去上课，属合理却没说。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音末尾引了小号手 the great trumpet player, Louis Armstrong 的话来佐证\"自己去\n听\"，D 项却说要\"请教爵士乐手 Consult jazz musicians\"，盗用\"乐手\"这一角色，把\"引用\n一位乐手的名言\"偷换成\"去请教乐手\"。\n借⽤原⽂：As the great trumpet player, Louis Armstrong said, if you gotta ask, you'll never know", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What did the woman do this morning?", "options": ["A) She paid her mortgage.", "B) She called on the man.", "C) She made a business plan.", "D) She went to the bank."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She went to the bank.\n【定位】M: How did it go at the bank this morning? W: Not well, my proposal was rejected.\n男：你今早在银行进展如何？女：不顺利，我的申请被拒了。\n【信号】对话开场的一问一答。男方一开口就问 How did it go at the bank this morning?，把\n「今早」和「银行」直接绑在一起，女方紧接着应答 my proposal was rejected——她今\n早去做的事就藏在这句开场问答里：去了银行（并被拒）。对话题答案落在被问者的应答，\n这里开场问答即锁定动作。\n【替换】at the bank this morning ↔ went to the bank\n在银行=去了银行\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确实提到 paying my mortgage，但那是银行说「再还五年按揭」才更有资格，是\n未来条件、不是她今早做的事；选项 She paid her mortgage. 盗用 mortgage 这个原词制\n造「听到了就想选」的诱惑，却答错了「今早做什么」。\n借⽤原⽂：after five more years of paying my mortgage\nB) 未提及\n对话里两人在交谈，但录音从没说她「今早去拜访了这个男的（called on the man）」；\n这是凭「两人在对话」想当然，原文并无对应事实。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音男方问 Did you show them your business plan?，business plan 确被念到，但「做\n商业计划书」不是她今早做的事，今早做的是带着计划去银行被拒；She made a business\nplan. 借 business plan 这个原词诱选，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Did you show them your business plan?", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Why was the woman's proposal rejected?", "options": ["A) Her previous debt hadn't been cleared yet.", "B) Her credit history was considered poor.", "C) She had apparently asked for too much.", "D) She didn't pay her mortgage in time."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Her credit history was considered poor.\n【定位】W: Bunch of reasons. For starters, they said my credit history was not good enough.\n女：一堆原因。首先，他们说我的信用记录不够好。\n【信号】they said 引出银行明说的理由。女方用 they said my credit history was not good\nenough 直接转述银行给出的理由，they said 是「明示理由」的强信号；后文 looked at\nmy credit history and determined it was not good enough 再次确认，理由就是信用记\n录不达标。\n【替换】my credit history was not good enough ↔ Her credit history was considered poor.\nnot good enough=poor\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音后文 how much debt I have、50% less debt 确实念到 debt，但那是她将来要改善\n的目标，不是「旧债没清」导致被拒；Her previous debt hadn't been cleared yet. 借\ndebt 诱选，且银行明说的理由是信用记录而非旧债未清。\n借⽤原⽂：how much debt I have\nC) 与原文相反\n男方建议要小额时，女方明确说 It doesn't matter the size of the loan I ask for——金额\n大小无关；可见被拒不是因为「要得太多」，She had apparently asked for too much.\n与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：It doesn't matter the size of the loan I ask for\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 paying my mortgage，但说的是「再还五年按揭会更有资格」，从没说她「没按\n时还按揭」；She didn't pay her mortgage in time. 盗用 mortgage 原词，编出一个原文\n未说的失误。\n借⽤原⽂：after five more years of paying my mortgage", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is the woman planning to do?", "options": ["A) Pay a debt long overdue", "B) Buy a piece of property", "C) Start her own business", "D) Check her credit history"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Start her own business\n【定位】If I want to continue ahead with this dream of owning my own business, I have no other\nchoice but to build up my own finances.\n如果我想继续追求拥有自己企业的梦想，我别无选择，只能先把自己的财力攒起来。\n【信号】I want to…dream of 表强烈意图。女方说 If I want to continue ahead with this dream\nof owning my own business，want to 加 dream of 直接道出她的长远打算就是「拥有自\n己的企业」；这是被问者亲口表达意图的句子，正是计划题的落点。\n【替换】this dream of owning my own business ↔ Start her own business\n拥有自己的企业=创办自己的企业\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音提到她要 50% less debt（减少负债），但「还一笔早该还的债（pay a debt long\noverdue）」是把「减债目标」偷换成「还某笔逾期债」，且这是改善财力的手段、不是她\n说的核心计划；Pay a debt long overdue 借 debt 概念诱选。\n借⽤原⽂：50% less debt\nB) 未提及\n录音从没说她计划「买房产（buy a piece of property）」；这是由前文 mortgage（按\n揭）联想出的置业话题，属脑补，原文无据。\nD) 原词陷阱\ncredit history 在录音里多次出现（my credit history），但「查自己的信用记录（check\nher credit history）」既非她说的计划、原文也没说她要去查；选项盗用 credit history 原\n词诱选。\n借⽤原⽂：They simply looked at my credit history", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the man suggest the woman do?", "options": ["A) Seek advice from an expert about fundraising.", "B) Ask for smaller loans from different lenders.", "C) Build up her own finances step by step.", "D) Revise her business proposal carefully."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Ask for smaller loans from different lenders.\n【定位】M: What if you ask for a smaller amount? Maybe you could gather capital from other sources.\nSmaller loans from more lenders?\n男：要是你申请小一点的金额呢？也许你可以从别的渠道筹集资金。向更多放款方借更小额\n的贷款？\n【信号】What if you… / Maybe you could 建议句。男方用 What if you ask for a smaller\namount? 和 Maybe you could gather capital from other sources 提出建议，What if\nyou、Maybe you could 是典型的建议信号；题干问的是「男方建议什么」，落点就在这\n句，注意不要被女方随后的反驳带走。\n【替换】Smaller loans from more lenders? ↔ Ask for smaller loans from different lenders.\nmore lenders=different lenders\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n男方说 gather capital from other sources（从别处筹资），选项 Seek advice from an\nexpert about fundraising. 把「自己筹资」偷换成「向专家请教融资」，并加了原文没有\n的 an expert；男方从没建议去找专家。\n借⽤原⽂：gather capital from other sources\nC) 张冠李戴\n「一步步攒自己财力（build up her own finances）」确实在录音里，但说这话的是女方 I\nhave no other choice but to build up my own finances，不是男方的建议；题干问男方\n建议，把女方的结论安到男方头上就错了。\n借⽤原⽂：build up my own finances\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里有 business plan 和 proposal 等词，但没人建议「仔细修改商业计划书（revise\nher business proposal carefully）」；选项盗用 proposal 这个原词，编出一个谁都没提\n的建议。\n借⽤原⽂：Did you show them your business plan?", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about Leo Sanchez's farm?", "options": ["A) It is profitable and environmentally friendly.", "B) It is well located and completely automated.", "C) It is small and unconventional.", "D) It is fertile and productive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is small and unconventional.\n【定位】There's a lot about Leo Sanchez and his farm in Salinas, California, that seems unusual. The\nnational average farm size is around 440 acres, but his is only one acre.\n关于 Leo Sanchez 和他在加州 Salinas 的农场，有很多地方显得不寻常。全国农场平均规\n模约 440 英亩，但他的只有 1 英亩。\n【信号】评价词 unusual + 对比转折 but。录音先抛出评价词 seems unusual（显得不寻常），紧\n接着用对比句 but his is only one acre 把“平均 440 英亩”与“他只有 1 英亩”一比，转折\nbut 之后正是答案落点——又小又非常规。\n【替换】his is only one acre ↔ small\n具体数字→概括形容词\nseems unusual ↔ unconventional\n同义词替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说农场 profitable and environmentally friendly（盈利又环保），录音第 2 段确实提到\n他经营网售、不受大公司套路约束，听感上沾边，但录音并没说这个农场“赚钱且环保”，是\n凭印象拼凑出来的。\n借⽤原⽂：the promotion and sale of his produce online\nB) 未提及\nB 说农场 completely automated（完全自动化），但全篇讲的是他用手动农具、火焰除草\n器等大小工具，根本没说农场“完全自动化”，这是无中生有，且 completely 是预读时就该\n警惕的绝对词。\nD) 未提及\nD 说农场 fertile and productive（肥沃高产），这是人们对农场的常识想象，录音却只字\n未提土地肥沃或产量高，是用常识替代文本的误选。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What has motivated Leo Sanchez and his fellow young farmers to engage in farming?", "options": ["A) Their urge to make farming more enjoyable.", "B) Their desire to improve farming equipment.", "C) Their hope to revitalize traditional farming.", "D) Their wish to set a new farming standard."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） Their wish to set a new farming standard.\n【定位】Sanchez says he and many of his fellow young farmers are motivated by desire to set a new\nstandard for agriculture.\nSanchez 说，他和许多年轻农民同行是被“为农业树立新标准”的愿望所驱动。\n【信号】动机标志词 motivated by desire to。录音直接出现题目要找的标志词组 motivated by\ndesire to set a new standard，motivated by/desire to 就是动机信号，这一句即答案\n句，无需多猜。\n【替换】set a new standard for agriculture ↔ set a new farming standard\n近义词替换 agriculture=farming\nare motivated by desire to ↔ Their wish to\ndesire=wish 同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说动机是 make farming more enjoyable（让耕作更有趣），录音第 3 段反而说务农\ndifficult for both the body and mind（身心都辛苦），并未把“更有趣”当动机，属无中生\n有。\nB) 答非所问\nB 说动机是 improve farming equipment（改进农具），录音第 4 段确实大谈各种工具，\n听到 tool/equipment 很容易顺手选，但那是讲他“用什么工具”，不是讲他务农的“动机”，\n答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：employing a multitude of technologies\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说动机是 revitalize traditional farming（复兴传统农业），而录音明说动机是 set a\nnew standard（树立新标准）——一个朝“旧传统”、一个朝“新标准”，方向正相反，是正话\n反说的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：set a new standard for agriculture", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why did Leo Sanchez buy a hand-operated weeding tool?", "options": ["A) It saves a lot of electricity.", "B) It needs little maintenance.", "C) It causes hardly any pollution.", "D) It loosens soil while weeding."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It loosens soil while weeding.\n【定位】Recently, Sanchez bought a hand-operated tool which pulls out weeds and loosens soil.\n最近，Sanchez 买了一件手动工具，它能拔除杂草并疏松土壤。\n【信号】工具后紧跟的功能定语 which pulls out weeds and loosens soil。录音在 bought a\nhand-operated tool 后立刻用定语从句 which pulls out weeds and loosens soil 说明它\n能做什么，这个紧跟工具的功能描述就是答案信号——它干两件事：除草+松土。\n【替换】pulls out weeds and loosens soil ↔ loosens soil while weeding\n并列动作→while 同时表述\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说它 saves a lot of electricity（省很多电），但录音里手动工具是 hand-operated（手\n动的）、另一件是 gas-powered（燃气动力），全篇没说它省电，省电是凭“手动＝省电”的\n常识脑补出来的。\nB) 未提及\nB 说它 needs little maintenance（几乎不用维护），录音对这件手动工具只讲了功能和它\n源自 1701 年，完全没提维护问题，属无中生有。\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说它 causes hardly any pollution（几乎不污染），但录音里另一件 gas-powered\nflame weedkiller（燃气火焰除草器）才与污染话题相关，手动工具并没说无污染；把另一\n件工具/常识印象安到这件上，是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：a gas-powered flame weedkiller", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the food company Eat Grub?", "options": ["A) It has turned certain insects into a new food source.", "B) It has started to expand business outside the UK.", "C) It has imported some exotic foods from overseas.", "D) It has joined hands with Sainsbury's to sell pet insects."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has turned certain insects into a new food source.\n【定位】Eat Grub is Britain's first new food company that breaks western food boundaries by\nintroducing edible insects as a new source of food.\nEat Grub 是英国第一家打破西方饮食界限、把可食用昆虫作为一种新食物来源引入的新食\n品公司。\n【信号】开篇定义句即答案句。篇章开头第一句就给公司下定义：introducing edible insects as a\nnew source of food，独白体裁里“开篇定义/点题句”往往直接承载首题答案，听到“某公司\nis…”就要锁定。\n【替换】introducing edible insects as a new source of food ↔\nturned certain insects into a new food source\n同义改写：introducing…as=turned…into\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音只说 Eat Grub 是 Britain's first new food company，从未提它要把生意拓展到英国\n以外，expand business outside the UK 是凭空添加。\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说的是 introducing edible insects（引入可食用昆虫），imported some exotic\nfoods from overseas 把“引入昆虫”偷换成“从海外进口异国食品”，原文既没说 import 也\n没说 overseas，是借“introducing”语感设的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：introducing edible insects as a new source of food\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 Sainsbury's is the first UK supermarket to stock the company's crunchy\nroasted crickets，Sainsbury's 只是上架销售蟋蟀的超市，并非和公司合伙卖“宠物昆虫”；\njoined hands with Sainsbury's to sell pet insects 盗用了真实出现的 Sainsbury's，又把\n“供人吃”歪曲成“pet insects 宠物昆虫”。\n借⽤原⽂：Sainsbury's is the first UK supermarket to stock the company's crunchy roasted crickets", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about his first bite of roasted crickets?", "options": ["A) It was really unforgettable.", "B) It was a pleasant surprise.", "C) It hurt his throat slightly.", "D) It made him feel strange."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） It was a pleasant surprise.\n【定位】But the first bite was a pleasant surprise.\n但第一口竟是个令人愉快的惊喜。\n【信号】转折 But 之后才是真感受。前句铺垫“the idea…made me feel a little sick”是先抑，转折\n词 But the first bite was a pleasant surprise 后才是说话人对第一口的真实评价，态度题\n答案常落在 but 之后。\n【替换】the first bite was a pleasant surprise ↔ It was a pleasant surprise.\n几乎原词对应：a pleasant surprise\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从未说第一口“unforgettable”，It was really unforgettable. 是另造的强烈评价，与原\n文 a pleasant surprise 不是一回事。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说 at least a wing didn't get stuck in my throat——翅膀“没有”卡到喉咙，It hurt\nhis throat slightly. 盗用 throat 一词，却把“没卡到”反说成“弄伤了喉咙”，极性相反。\n借⽤原⽂：at least a wing didn't get stuck in my throat\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音里“觉得不适”是在吃之前——the idea of one going in my mouth made me feel a\nlittle sick，It made him feel strange. 把“吃前一想到就有点恶心”移花接木成“第一口让他\n觉得怪”，张冠李戴到了“first bite”上，而第一口其实是惊喜。\n借⽤原⽂：the idea of one going in my mouth made me feel a little sick", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does Eat Grub say about its dried crickets?", "options": ["A) They are more tasty than beef, chicken or pork.", "B) They are more nutritious than soups and salads.", "C) They contain more protein than conventional meats.", "D) They will soon gain popularity throughout the world."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They contain more protein than conventional meats.\n【定位】The company boasts that its dried crickets contain more protein than beef, chicken and pork,\nas well as minerals like iron and calcium.\n该公司夸耀说，它的干蟋蟀所含蛋白质比牛肉、鸡肉和猪肉都多，还含有铁、钙等矿物质。\n【信号】boast 引出公司的宣称。The company boasts that its dried crickets contain more\nprotein than beef, chicken and pork，boasts（夸耀）正对应题干“say about”，是公司\n声称的核心信息，听到 boasts / claims 这类“声称”动词就锁定其后内容。\n【替换】more protein than beef, chicken and pork ↔\nThey contain more protein than conventional meats\n上位概括：beef/chicken/pork=conventional meats\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 beef, chicken and pork 是和“protein 蛋白质”作比较，They are more tasty than\nbeef, chicken or pork. 盗用这三种肉，却把比较点偷换成“tasty 好吃”，原文谈的是营养不\n是口味。\n借⽤原⽂：more protein than beef, chicken and pork\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音里 Eat Grub also recommends the crickets as a topping for noodles, soups and\nsalads，soups and salads 是“蟋蟀可撒在上面”的食物，并非比较营养的对象；more\nnutritious than soups and salads 盗用这两个词，编出“比汤和沙拉更营养”的对比。\n借⽤原⽂：Eat Grub also recommends the crickets as a topping for noodles, soups and salads\nD) 与原文相反\n录音对“会否流行”其实是存疑的——I'm not sure bugs will become a popular snack\nanytime soon，They will soon gain popularity throughout the world. 既不是公司\nboast 的内容，方向也与原文的迟疑相反。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm not sure bugs will become a popular snack anytime soon", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say about insect farming?", "options": ["A) It is environmentally friendly.", "B) It is a promising industry.", "C) It requires new technology.", "D) It saves huge amounts of labour."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is environmentally friendly.\n【定位】Unlike the production of meat, bugs do not use up large amounts of land, water or feed. And\ninsect farming also produces far fewer greenhouse gases.\n与肉类生产不同，养虫不会消耗大量的土地、水或饲料。而且昆虫养殖排放的温室气体也少\n得多。\n【信号】对比 Unlike + 少排温室气体=环保。Unlike the production of meat, bugs do not use\nup large amounts of land, water or feed 用 Unlike 对比养虫“省资源”，再加 produces\nfar fewer greenhouse gases“少排温室气体”，两句合起来落在“环保”这一维度。\n【替换】do not use up large amounts of land, water or feed ↔ It is environmentally friendly.\n具体表现概括为“环保”\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n录音结尾说 I'm not sure bugs will become a popular snack anytime soon，对前景是保\n留态度，并未称养虫是“a promising industry”，It is a promising industry. 是过度拔高的\n推断。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm not sure bugs will become a popular snack anytime soon\nC) 未提及\n录音通篇没提养虫“requires new technology”，It requires new technology. 完全没有原\n文支撑。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说养虫不耗 land, water or feed（土地、水、饲料），It saves huge amounts of\nlabour. 借“省…”的语感，却把省的对象偷换成 labour（人力），原文并未提到节省劳动\n力。\n借⽤原⽂：bugs do not use up large amounts of land, water or feed", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "Why do psychologists and educators study learning styles?", "options": ["A) To categorize different types of learners.", "B) To find out what students prefer to learn.", "C) To understand the mechanism of the human brain.", "D) To see if they are inherent traits affecting learning."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To see if they are inherent traits affecting learning.\n【定位】When psychologists and educators test for learning styles, they're trying to figure out whether\nthese are inherent traits that affect how well students learn, instead of just a preference.\n当心理学家和教育者检验学习风格时，他们想弄清楚这些到底是影响学生学习好坏的与生俱\n来的特质，还是仅仅是一种偏好。\n【信号】目的信号 trying to figure out。they're trying to figure out whether these are\ninherent traits 中 trying to figure out（试图弄清）正是点明研究目的的信号词，紧跟其\n后的 whether 从句就是答案内容，顺着这个目的标记锁定即可。\n【替换】whether these are inherent traits that affect how well students learn ↔\nif they are inherent traits affecting learning\n几乎原义保留的同义压缩\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 is there really a way to categorize different types of students 确实念到\ncategorize，但那只是讲者抛出的疑问，不是心理学家做研究的目的；To categorize\ndifferent types of learners. 抓了原词却答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：is there really a way to categorize different types of students\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 giving a survey to figure out what style a student favors 提到调查学生偏好，但那\n是研究的步骤之一，目的恰恰是要超越偏好去看是否为天生特质；To find out what\nstudents prefer to learn. 把手段误当成了目的。\n借⽤原⽂：figure out what style a student favors\nC) 未提及\nTo understand the mechanism of the human brain. 听上去很像心理学研究的口吻，但\n录音通篇讲的是学习风格是否为天生特质，从未把理解大脑机制当作研究目的，属无中生\n有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker say about one study mentioned in the 2008 review?", "options": ["A) It was defective.", "B) It was misguided.", "C) It was original in design.", "D) It was thought-provoking."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was defective.\n【定位】But the study had some big flaws. The researchers excluded two-thirds of the original\nparticipants because they didn't seem to have any clear learning style from the survey at the\nbeginning. And, they didn't even report the actual test scores in the final paper.\n但这项研究有一些大问题。研究者剔除了三分之二的原始被试，因为他们在最初的问卷里看\n不出有任何清晰的学习风格。而且，他们在最终论文里甚至没有报告真实的测试分数。\n【信号】转折 But + 负面词 big flaws。But the study had some big flaws 一句以 But 转折，紧\n跟 big flaws（大缺陷）这一明确负面评价，转折后才是讲者真正想说的态度，正是答案句\n信号。\n【替换】the study had some big flaws ↔ It was defective.\nflaws 与 defective 同义\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nIt was misguided.（被误导的/方向错的）虽同为贬义，但原文批的是研究设计上的具体缺\n陷（剔人、不报分），并未说它整体方向被引偏；同是负面也要选与原文最贴的那种负面。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt was original in design. 说设计有原创性，是褒义，与原文 big flaws 的负面定调完全相\n反；这是把贬义听成褒义的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：had some big flaws\nD) 未提及\nIt was thought-provoking.（发人深省）是正面评价，原文从头到尾只在数落这项研究的\n毛病，从未夸它启发性，属无中生有的褒义干扰。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What message does the speaker want to convey about learning at the end of the talk?", "options": ["A) Auditory aids are as important as visual aids.", "B) Visual aids are helpful to all types of learners.", "C) Reading plain texts is more effective than viewing pictures.", "D) Scientific concepts are hard to understand without visual aids."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Visual aids are helpful to all types of learners.\n【定位】Instead, most people seem to learn better if they're taught in several ways—especially if one\nis visual. In one study, researchers tested whether students remembered lists of words better if\nthey heard them, saw them, or both. And everyone seemed to do better if they got to see the\nwords in print, even the self-identified auditory learners.\n相反，多数人若以多种方式被教授、尤其其中一种是视觉方式时，似乎学得更好。在一项研\n究里，研究者检验学生是听到、看到还是两者兼有时更能记住词表。结果每个人在能看到印\n出的词时似乎都表现更好，连自认为是听觉型的学习者也不例外。\n【信号】收束词 Instead + 全称 everyone。结尾段以 Instead, most people seem to learn\nbetter 收束全文，再用 everyone seemed to do better if they got to see the words in\nprint, even the self-identified auditory learners 中的 everyone、even 把视觉辅助的有\n效性推及所有人，正是主旨落点。\n【替换】everyone seemed to do better if they got to see the words in print, even the self-identified auditory\nlearners\n↔ Visual aids are helpful to all types of learners.\neveryone+even 概括为 all types\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nAuditory aids are as important as visual aids. 说听觉与视觉同等重要，但原文恰恰强调\neveryone seemed to do better if they got to see the words in print——视觉更胜一\n筹，连听觉型也受益于视觉，并非二者并列，属与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：everyone seemed to do better if they got to see the words in print\nC) 原词陷阱\nReading plain texts is more effective than viewing pictures. 称读纯文字比看图更有效，\n而原文 everyone did better with the help of pictures 明说看图更好，正好相反；它盗用\n了 plain text、pictures 两个原词却把胜负说颠倒了。\n借⽤原⽂：reading plain text or viewing pictures, too. And everyone did better with the help\nof pictures\nD) 过度推断\nScientific concepts are hard to understand without visual aids. 说没视觉辅助科学概念\n就难懂，原文只是说看图能帮学得更好（learned basic physics and chemistry concepts\nbetter by reading plain text or viewing pictures），从更好被夸大成离了它就不行，属\n过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：learned basic physics and chemistry concepts better by reading plain text or\nviewing pictures", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do people often feel guilty about according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Not playing a role in a workplace revolution.", "B) Not benefiting from free-market capitalism.", "C) Not earning enough money to provide for the family.", "D) Not spending enough time on family life and leisure."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Not spending enough time on family life and leisure.\n【定位】We may instead find ourselves feeling guilty about the time we didn't spend watching our\nchildren grow, or with our loved ones, or travelling, or on the cultural or leisure pursuits that\nbring us happiness.\n我们反倒可能会为这些时间感到内疚：没能陪伴孩子成长、没能与所爱之人相处、没去旅\n行、没去从事那些带给我们幸福的文化或休闲活动。\n【信号】feeling guilty 原词直接领出答案。录音里 feeling guilty about the time we didn't\nspend 与题干 guilty 完全对应，是直接应答信号；紧跟其后的 with our loved ones, or\ntravelling, or on the cultural or leisure pursuits 就是内疚的具体内容。\n【替换】the time we didn't spend watching our children grow, or with our loved ones ↔\nNot spending enough time on family life\n陪孩子与亲人 概括成 family life\ntravelling, or on the cultural or leisure pursuits ↔ and leisure\n旅行与文化追求 归并为 leisure\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 embrace a workplace revolution，但那是讲\"应当拥抱职场变革\"，不是人们内\n疚的内容；选项把这个耳熟的词搬来制造\"听到就想选\"。\n借⽤原⽂：It's imperative for us to embrace a workplace revolution.\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音开篇 Free market capitalism hasn't freed us 谈的是资本主义没带来自由，并非\"人们\n为没从中受益而内疚\"，选项把这个高频词嫁接到\"内疚\"上。\n借⽤原⽂：Free market capitalism hasn't freed us; it has trapped us.\nC) 未提及\n录音后文虽提到 rising personal debt 等经济困境，但从未说人们\"为没挣够钱养家\"而内\n疚；内疚句明确落在时间与陪伴上，C 属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did leading economists predict 90 years ago?", "options": ["A) People would be working only fifteen hours a week now.", "B) The balance of power in the workplace would change.", "C) Technological advances would create many new jobs.", "D) Most workers could afford to have a house of their own"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） People would be working only fifteen hours a week now.\n【定位】Nine decades ago, leading economists predicted that technological advances and rising\nproductivity would mean that we'd be working a 15-hour week by now: that target has been\nsomewhat missed.\n九十年前，顶尖经济学家曾预测，技术进步和生产率提高将意味着我们到现在每周只需工作\n15 小时：这个目标多少有些落空了。\n【信号】predicted 直接领出预测内容。录音 leading economists predicted that... 与题干\npredict 完全对上，predicted 后面的 that 从句就是预测内容；其中 we'd be working a\n15-hour week by now 是关键数字。\n【替换】we'd be working a 15-hour week by now ↔\nPeople would be working only fifteen hours a week now\n15-hour 写成 fifteen hours 几乎照搬\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nthe balance of power in the workplace has been shifted 是说话人自己对现状的描述\n（第4段），不是九十年前经济学家的预测；选项把第4段的词搬到第3段的预测上。\n借⽤原⽂：the balance of power in the workplace has been shifted so dramatically from worker\nto boss\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音预测里确有 technological advances，但接的是\"工时降到15小时\"，并非\"创造许多新\n岗位\"；选项盗用这个词却换成录音没说的结论。\n借⽤原⽂：technological advances and rising productivity would mean that we'd be working a 15-\nhour week by now\nD) 未提及\n录音第3段提到 secure, affordable housing 的缺失，是说现状住房没保障，并非经济学家\n预测\"大多数工人能买得起自己的房\"；D 属未提及的预测。\n借⽤原⽂：the lack of secure, affordable housing", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What is the result of denying workers' right to make their own choices?", "options": ["A) Loss of workers' personal dignity.", "B) Deprivation of workers' creativity.", "C) Deterioration of workers' mental health.", "D) Unequal distribution of working hours."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Deterioration of workers' mental health.\n【定位】It's time in which we are directed by the needs and desires of others, and denied the right to\nmake our own choices. That's bad for us: it's hardly surprising that over half a million\nworkers suffer from work-related mental health conditions each year.\n在这段时间里，我们被他人的需求和欲望所支配，被剥夺了自主选择的权利。这对我们有\n害：难怪每年有超过五十万工人遭受与工作相关的心理健康问题。\n【信号】denied the right 后接 That's bad 因果落点。录音先复述题干的原因 denied the right\nto make our own choices，紧接 That's bad for us 是因果信号，其后 suffer from work-\nrelated mental health conditions 就是结果。\n【替换】suffer from work-related mental health conditions ↔ Deterioration of workers' mental health.\n遭受心理疾病=心理健康恶化\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音说被剥夺 freedom and personal autonomy（自由与自主），选项却换成 personal\ndignity（人格尊严），偷换了概念，且\"尊严受损\"并非这句给出的具体结果。\n借⽤原⽂：the surrender of our freedom and personal autonomy\nB) 未提及\n录音通篇未提工人的\"创造力\"（creativity），这是凭\"职场压抑\"的印象想当然加上的，属未\n提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nredistribute hours from the overworked to the underworked 确实谈到工时分配，但那\n是后文讲解决办法的内容，不是\"剥夺选择权\"的结果；选项搬来 working hours 制造熟词诱\n惑。\n借⽤原⽂：redistribute hours from the overworked to the underworked", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the dream airport in Berlin?", "options": ["A) It is the worst managed airport in German history.", "B) It is now the biggest and busiest airport in Europe.", "C) It has become something of a joke among Germans.", "D) It has become a typical symbol of German efficiency."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） It has become something of a joke among Germans.\n【定位】Plagued by long delays, perpetual mismanagement and ever-soaring costs, the airport has\nbecome something of a joke among Germans—and a source of frustration for local\npoliticians, business leaders and residents alike.\n由于长期延误、长年管理不善以及不断飙升的成本，这座机场已经成了德国人眼中的一个笑\n话——也成了当地政界人士、商界领袖和居民共同的烦恼之源。\n【信号】贬义评价词直接亮明态度。答案句出现强烈的负面评价词 a joke among Germans 和 a\nsource of frustration，这类带感情色彩的定性词就是态度题的答案信号，听到立即锁定。\n【替换】the airport has become something of a joke among Germans ↔\nIt has become something of a joke among Germans.\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】A)\n选项 the worst managed airport in German history 极性虽与录音一致（也是负面），但\n录音只说管理不善 perpetual mismanagement，从未说「德国史上最差」，属把程度夸\n大、无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：perpetual mismanagement\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 the biggest and busiest airport in Europe 盗用了录音里 every other major\nmodern airport in Europe 的 airport / Europe 等原词，但录音说这机场「至今空置 still\nstands empty」，根本不是最大最忙，极性正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：every other major modern airport in Europe\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 a typical symbol of German efficiency 盗用录音 Germany is known for its\nefficiency 的 efficiency 一词，但录音紧接着说「但说到这座机场，这名声远非如此 this\nreputation could not be further from the truth」，机场恰恰是效率的反面，故 D 与原\n意相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Germany is known for its efficiency", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the dream airport in Berlin?", "options": ["A) The city's airports are outdated.", "B) The city had just been reunified.", "C) The city wanted to boost its economy.", "D) The city wanted to attract more tourists."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The city had just been reunified.\n【定位】At the time, it became clear that the newly-reunified Berlin would need a modern airport with\nfar greater capacity than its existing airports.\n当时，情况已经很清楚：刚刚实现重新统一的柏林，需要一座容量远超现有机场的现代化\n机场。\n【信号】背景时间状语点出原因。答案句以 At the time 引出背景，关键信息落在 the newly-\nreunified Berlin——「刚统一的柏林」正是建机场的背景；这类交代背景的时间/状态短语\n就是细节答案的信号。\n【替换】the newly-reunified Berlin ↔ The city had just been reunified.\n形容词转成完成句\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 The city's airports are outdated 盗用录音 its existing airports 的 airports 一词，\n但录音说的是新机场要「容量远大于」现有机场 far greater capacity than its existing\nairports，强调的是容量需求，并没说旧机场「过时 outdated」，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：far greater capacity than its existing airports\nC)\n选项 The city wanted to boost its economy 是常识里建大机场顺理成章的动机，但录音\n讲背景时只提「容量需求 + 刚统一」，全程没说为了提振经济，属未提及。\nD)\n选项 The city wanted to attract more tourists 同样是凭常识脑补的动机，录音的背景说\n明里 would need a modern airport with far greater capacity 只谈容量，没有任何「吸\n引游客」的内容，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why did Berlin postpone the opening of its dream airport again and again?", "options": ["A) The municipal government kept changing hands.", "B) The construction firm breached the contract.", "C) Shortage of funding delayed its construction.", "D) Problems of different kinds kept popping up."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Problems of different kinds kept popping up.\n【定位】It wasn't just the smoke system: many other major problems subsequently emerged. More\nthan 90 metres of cable were incorrectly installed; 4,000 doors were wrongly numbered;\nescalators were too short. And there was a shortage of check-in desks.\n问题不止是排烟系统：随后还冒出许多别的重大问题。九十多米的线缆装错了；四千扇门编\n号弄错了；自动扶梯太短了。此外还缺少值机柜台。\n【信号】一连串不同问题的罗列。答案句把毛病一项项铺开——cable were incorrectly installed、\ndoors were wrongly numbered、escalators were too short、a shortage of check-in\ndesks，这种「多类问题接连出现」的罗列，正是指向「各种问题层出不穷」这一概括答案\n的信号。\n【替换】many other major problems subsequently emerged ↔\nProblems of different kinds kept popping up.\n概括对概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 The municipal government kept changing hands 盗用录音 Leadership of the\nplanning corporation has changed hands 的 changed hands，但录音说的是「规划公\n司的领导层」频繁更替，不是「市政府」易主，主体被张冠李戴，且这是延误的伴随现象而\n非题问的核心原因。\n借⽤原⽂：Leadership of the planning corporation has changed hands\nB)\n选项 The construction firm breached the contract 借录音里反复出现的 construction\ncorporation 之熟词诱选，但录音从没说施工方「违约 breached the contract」，只说公\n司自己缺乏经验去管理项目，属未提及。\n借⽤原⽂：appointing a general contractor\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Shortage of funding delayed its construction 盗用录音里 a shortage of check-in\ndesks 的 shortage 一词，但录音说的是「缺值机柜台」「成本飙升」，钱是越花越多，从\n没说「资金短缺导致延误」，属偷换概念、甚至与原文方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：there was a shortage of check-in desks", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What happens while the airport remains unused?", "options": ["A) Tourism industry in Berlin suffers.", "B) All kinds of equipment gets rusted.", "C) Huge maintenance costs accumulate.", "D) Complaints by local residents increase."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Huge maintenance costs accumulate.\n【定位】To compound the delays, the unused airport is resulting in massive costs. Every month that it\nremains unopened, it costs between €9m and €10m.\n雪上加霜的是，这座闲置的机场还在持续产生巨额成本。它每空置一个月，就要花费九百万\n到一千万欧元。\n【信号】成本词加金额数字。答案句出现 massive costs 和具体金额 between €9m and €10m，\n预读时圈定的『成本/数字』信号在此兑现——听到 costs 加钱数，立即锁定这是 25 的答\n案句。\n【替换】the unused airport is resulting in massive costs ↔ Huge maintenance costs accumulate.\n巨额成本同义改写\nEvery month that it remains unopened, it costs between €9m and €10m ↔ accumulate\n按月累计=累积\n【排除】A)\n选项 Tourism industry in Berlin suffers 是『机场不开→旅游业受损』的常识联想，录音讲\n闲置后果时只谈成本，全程没提旅游业，属未提及。\nB)\n选项 All kinds of equipment gets rusted 听起来像『空置久了会锈』的合理想象，但录音\n里讲到的设备问题是装错、太短、缺柜台（escalators were too short），从没说『生\n锈』，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Complaints by local residents increase 盗用录音 a source of frustration for local\npoliticians, business leaders and residents 里的 residents，但那是在讲机场总体形象时\n提到居民感到沮丧，并非『闲置期间投诉增多』这一后果，且没说投诉『增加』，属张冠李\n戴。\n借⽤原⽂：a source of frustration for local politicians, business leaders and residents alike", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) prematurely\n副词\n【词性槽】完整句缺修饰语，需副词修饰动词 died\n【依据】『过早地』正契合污染致死语境\n上文 The persistent haze over many of our cities is a reminder of the polluted air\nthat we breathe 在讲污染危害，450 万人因户外污染死亡，强调的是『本不该这么早就死\n却被污染夺命』，prematurely（过早地）正好刻画这种非自然提前死亡。\n【竞争词】L) simply 『仅仅』与死因无逻辑关联\nsimply 意为『仅仅/简单地』，放进 die simply from… 语义空洞，与统计 450 万死亡的严肃语\n境不搭，且 simply 在第 35 题另有更贴的搭配位（almost double → 几乎），应留给那道空。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·完整句缺修饰语，需副词修饰动词 died"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) determine\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 will 后需动词原形\n【依据】『决定』未来生活与健康\nthe decisions that we make now about the design of our cities will __(27)__ the\neveryday lives and health of the coming generations：『今天的城市设计决定未来几代\n人的日常生活与健康』，determine（决定/影响走向）正是这种因果定调；与前句 What is\nmore important 也呼应——重要在于现在的决策对未来的『决定性』作用。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 will 后需动词原形"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) synonymous\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 become 之后需表语形容词\n【依据】be synonymous with = 与……几乎等同\nTraffic has become __(28)__ with air pollution, and many countries intend to ban the\nsale of petrol and diesel cars 表达『交通已与空气污染密不可分』，这正是 synonymous\nwith 的核心义——两件事在大众认知里几乎划等号；下半句各国禁售燃油车恰是这种『等\n号印象』的政策回应。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·系动词 become 之后需表语形容词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) switching\n动词\n【词性槽】副词 simply 后需动名词，作主语\n【依据】switch to electric cars = 改用电动车，是新闻地道搭配\nBut simply __(29)__ to electric cars will not mean pollution-free cities：上文刚说各国\n要禁售燃油车，此处转折『仅仅换成电动车也未必无污染』，switch to（改用/切换到）正\n是描述能源类型转换的标准动词，与下文电力来源、轮胎刹车颗粒物的讨论形成逻辑链。\n【竞争词】H) opting 搭配错位：opt for 而非 opt to + 名词\nopt 表『选择』时固定搭配是 opt for sth 或 opt to do sth，而不是 opt to + 名词。这里空后\n是 to electric cars（介词 to + 名词），与 opt 的语法框架不符。opting 应留给第 32 题的 are\nopting for other means of travel。\nE) locating 义不对：『定位』与切换车型无关\nlocating 意为『确定位置/选址』，与『换成电动车』的语义毫不相干；且 locate 通常作及物动\n词直接接宾语，不与 to 构成习惯搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— opting 形态对，却被介词 to 卡死。29 题 But simply\n__(29)__ to electric cars 看到 -ing 词形和『选择电动车』的语义诱惑，容易直接抓\nopting；但 opt 搭配是 opt for sth，不是 opt to + 名词，正确答案是 switching（switch\nto）。防坑法：动词搭配看准 for/to/from/of 等介词，一字之差就错。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词 simply 后需动名词，作主语"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) generated\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后需过去分词，构成被动\n【依据】electricity is generated 是『电是怎么发出来的』标准说法\nThe level of emissions they cause will depend on how the electricity to run them is\n__(30)__：电动车的排放高低，取决于驱动它们的电是『怎么发出来的』——煤电、风电、\n核电决定排放——generate electricity（发电）是固定搭配，被动 electricity is\ngenerated 完全自然。\n【竞争词】F) merged 『合并』与发电主题不搭\nmerge 表『合并/融合』，多用于公司、文件、河流；说『电是怎么被合并的』在语义上不通，\n与上下文谈电力来源的污染影响完全无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 is 后需过去分词，构成被动"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) particles\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 + ___ 作宾语，需名词复数\n【依据】airborne particles = 空气悬浮颗粒，环境科学固定术语\n整段在讲电动车也未必让城市无污染：brakes, tyres and roads all create tiny airborne\n__(31)__ as they wear out，刹车片、轮胎和路面磨损会产生『微小的空气悬浮颗粒』——\n这正是大气科学里的 PM 颗粒物概念，与全文污染主题严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】G) miniatures 『微缩模型』与污染语境完全跑题\nminiatures 指『微型画/微缩模型』，多用于艺术、玩具语境；说刹车和轮胎磨损产生『微缩模\n型』在物理与语义上都讲不通，是命题人利用『miniature 看着像 mini』设的陷阱。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 + ___ 作宾语，需名词复数"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) opting\n动词\n【词性槽】are + ___ 进行时，且后接 for\n【依据】opt for = 选择，固定搭配\nyoung people especially are __(32)__ for other means of travel：年轻人尤其『正在选\n择其他出行方式』——opt for sth 正是英语里『选择某物/某条路径』的常见动词搭配，与\n全段年轻人减少开车、转向新出行方式的语境契合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·are + ___ 进行时，且后接 fo"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) peak\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词性物主代词 its 后需名词\n【依据】reach its peak 是固定搭配『达到顶峰』\nResearchers are already asking if motor vehicle use has reached its __(33)__ and will\ndecline：研究者在问机动车使用是否已『达到顶峰并即将下降』——peak 与紧随其后的\ndecline（下降）构成『顶峰→下降』的逻辑闭环，reach the/its peak 也是英语里最常见的\n搭配。\n【竞争词】O) trend 『达到趋势』搭配不成立且逻辑反转\nreach its trend 在英语里不是地道搭配；而且 trend 是『走向/潮流』，与紧跟的 will decline\n（即将下降）形成不了对照逻辑——下文要表达的是『到顶后下落』，不是『到达趋势』。\ntrend 应留给下题 34 题，其语境正是『这一趋势』。\nB) crown 义近 peak 但搭配不当\ncrown 虽有『顶点』之意，但 reach its crown 不是英语固定搭配；同时全段语气偏中性学术，\ncrown 文学色彩过浓，不如 peak 自然。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— crown『顶点』乍看可填，实为搭配陷阱。crown 在词典里\n也释为『顶点』，与 reached its __(33)__ and will decline 的『见顶』看似匹配；但 reach\nits crown 不是地道搭配，reach its peak 才是。防坑法：在词义『差不多』时，优先选择\n有固定搭配的那个词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词性物主代词 its 后需名词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) trend\n名词\n【词性槽】指示代词 this 后需名词\n【依据】catch up with this trend = 跟上这一趋势\n上一句刚说 Researchers are already asking if motor vehicle use has reached its\n__(33)__ and will decline，指明『机动车使用见顶下行』本身就是一个新出现的现象趋势；\nthis trend 正好回指上一句的『见顶下降』，与下文『不再修新路』形成对照。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·指示代词 this 后需名词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternate", "B) crown", "C) determine", "D) generated", "E) locating", "F) merged", "G) miniatures", "H) opting", "I) particles", "J) peak", "K) prematurely", "L) simply", "M) switching", "N) synonymous", "O) trend"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) simply\n副词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 与原形动词 double 之间需副词\n【依据】simply double = 简直就是翻倍\nstudies have shown that doubling the size of a road can __(35)__ double the traffic,\ntaking us back to the starting point：研究表明把路加倍，车流量也几乎『简直就翻\n倍』，simply 在此表示『简直/纯粹地』，强化『扩路反而毫无效果』的反讽，与结尾\ntaking us back to the starting point（回到原点）一拍即合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 can 与原形动词 doubl"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is quite easy for one to take in the recommended amount of protein.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“摄入推荐量”+“轻松”\n【定位】Add a lunch of chicken, rice and broccoli, and you are already over the recommended 50\ngrams. \"You can get enough protein and meet the RDA before you even get to dinner,\"\nsays Pipitone.\n再加上一顿鸡肉、米饭和西兰花的午餐，你就已经超过推荐的50克了。Pipitone说：“你在\n还没吃晚饭之前就能摄入足够蛋白质、达到RDA了。”\n【改写】take in the recommended amount of protein ↔ meet the RDA\n术语同义替换\nquite easy for one ↔ before you even get to dinner\n形容词→具体演示", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Pipitone claims that healthy adults need not spend money on protein supplements.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“Pipitone+健康成人+花钱”\n【定位】I've seen very expensive protein supplements that claim to be high quality but they might not\nreally be beneficial for the average healthy adult, she says. It could just be a waste of money.\n她说：“我见过非常昂贵、号称高品质的蛋白质补剂，但它们对一般健康成年人可能并没有\n真正的益处。”“它可能就只是浪费钱。”\n【改写】healthy adults ↔ the average healthy adult\n身份原样对应\nneed not spend money on protein supplements ↔ It could just be a waste of money\n反向判断同义\n【辨邻】 D D段Pipitone的论点是Whole foods are always the best option rather than adding\nsupplements——重点在比较“天然食物”和“补剂”哪个更好，而陈述句healthy adults\nneed not spend money on protein supplements的重点是“健康成人是否需要花钱\n买补剂”——前者讲选择，后者讲花钱，且D段没有healthy adults这一身份限定。\nWhole foods are always the best option rather than adding supplements", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The protein supplement business is found to be thriving.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“补剂业+蓬勃”\n【定位】With more than $12 billion in sales this year, the industry is booming and, according to the\nmarket research company, Grand View Research, is on track to sell billions more by 2025.\n今年销售额超过120亿美元，这个行业正在蓬勃发展，根据市场调研公司Grand View\nResearch的数据，到2025年还将再卖出数十亿美元。\n【改写】The protein supplement business ↔ the industry\nbusiness=industry\nthriving ↔ booming\nthriving=booming", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Protein can speed the repairing of damaged muscles.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“加速+修复肌肉”\n【定位】Extreme athletes expend lots of energy breaking down and repairing and building muscles.\nProtein can give them the edge they need to speed along that process.\n极限运动员消耗大量能量去分解、修复和构建肌肉。蛋白质能给他们所需的优势去加速这个\n过程。\n【改写】speed the repairing ↔ speed along that process\n动词+对象直接对应\ndamaged muscles ↔ breaking down and repairing and building muscles\n状态笼统化\n【辨邻】 H 陈述句speed the repairing of damaged muscles的关键是“加速修复受损肌肉”这一\n动作链，而H段讲的是significantly improved their muscle function——是“改善功\n能”，没有“加速修复”这一动作。\nthe high protein group had significantly improved their muscle function", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Protein supplements may overburden some internal organs, thus leading to their malfunctioning.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“内脏负担+功能失常”\n【定位】There have been some indications that extra protein makes the kidneys work harder, which\ncould be problematic for individuals with a history of kidney disease and for them, the\nsupplements may increase the risk of kidney stones, she says.\n有一些迹象表明，额外的蛋白质会让肾脏工作得更辛苦，这对有肾病史的人可能成问题，对\n他们而言，补剂可能增加得肾结石的风险。\n【改写】overburden some internal organs ↔ makes the kidneys work harder\n上下位词+动作同义\nleading to their malfunctioning ↔ increase the risk of kidney stones\n功能失常=器官疾病\n【辨邻】 L L段的痉挛、头痛、疲劳、脱水都属于全身性的不良反应，没有任何器官层面的描述，\n与陈述句overburden some internal organs的“使内脏负担过重”不构成对应。\ntoo much protein can cause cramps, headaches, and fatigue. Dehydration is also a risk", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Older adults need to take in more protein to keep their muscles strong.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“老年人+保肌”\n【定位】At around age 60, \"muscles really start to break down,\" says Kathryn Starr, an aging\nresearcher, \"and because of that, the protein needs of an older adult actually increase.\"\n老年研究者Kathryn Starr说：“60岁左右，肌肉真的开始分解了，正因为这样，老年人对蛋\n白质的需求实际上增加了。”\n【改写】Older adults ↔ an older adult\n身份原样对应\ntake in more protein ↔ the protein needs ... actually increase\n动作↔需求的等价\nkeep their muscles strong ↔ muscles really start to break down\n正向目的对应反向问题\n【辨邻】 H H段是一场具体实验的描述，对象是“想减重的肥胖老年人”这一更窄人群，且重点在\nthe high protein group had significantly improved their muscle function这一实\n验结果——而陈述句Q41问的是“老年人普遍需要多摄入蛋白”这一一般性论断，论断主\n体在G段。\nadding extra protein foods to the diet of obese older individuals who were trying to lose weight\nstrengthened their muscles\nK K段muscle strength is key to helping them stay strong and continue living on\ntheir own in their own home——讲的是肌力的意义，不是“老年人为什么要多吃\n蛋白”。\nmuscle strength is key to helping them stay strong and continue living on their own in their\nown home", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Protein is found in more foods than people might realize.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“分布广+超认知”\n【定位】But it's also in foods that may not come immediately to mind like vegetables, fruit, beans\nand grains.\n但蛋白质也存在于一些可能不会立刻想到的食物里，比如蔬菜、水果、豆类和谷物。\n【改写】more foods than people might realize ↔ a lot higher in protein than a lot of us think\n比较句式的同义改写\nmore foods ↔\nfoods that may not come immediately to mind like vegetables, fruit, beans and grains\n笼统范围对应具体列举\n【辨邻】 C C段You can get enough protein and meet the RDA before you even get to\ndinner讲的是“很容易就能达到推荐量”，重点在“数量”而不是“范围”；陈述句more\nfoods than people might realize问的是“分布范围超出认知”，论点角度不同。\nYou can get enough protein and meet the RDA before you even get to dinner", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Additional protein was found to help strengthen the muscles of overweight seniors seeking weight loss.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“超重老人+减重+加蛋白”\n【定位】Starr recently conducted a small study that found that adding extra protein foods to the diet of\nobese older individuals who were trying to lose weight strengthened their muscles.\nStarr最近做了一项小研究，发现给那些想减重的肥胖老年人在饮食中加入额外蛋白质食物，\n强化了他们的肌肉。\n【改写】overweight seniors seeking weight loss ↔\nobese older individuals who were trying to lose weight\n三重身份的同义词替换\nAdditional protein was found to help strengthen the muscles ↔\nadding extra protein foods to the diet ... strengthened their muscles\n动作直接对应\n【辨邻】 G G段虽然有“老年人”和“蛋白”，但缺少陈述句overweight和seeking weight loss这两\n个元素——陈述句明确指向“想减重的肥胖老年人”这一更窄人群，必须在H段才能完全\n对应。\nthe protein needs of an older adult actually increase", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Pipitone believes that whole foods provide the best source of protein.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“Pipitone+天然食物+最佳”\n【定位】No need to, says Pipitone because, in fact, most of us already get enough protein in our diet.\n\"Whole foods are always the best option rather than adding supplements,\" she says.\nPipitone说没必要，因为事实上我们大多数人已经从饮食中获得了足够的蛋白质。“天然食物\n始终是最好的选择，而不是加补剂。”\n【改写】Pipitone believes ↔ she says\n人名指代等价\nwhole foods provide the best source of protein ↔\nWhole foods are always the best option rather than adding supplements\n近义词替换+句式简化\n【辨邻】 E E段Pipitone的核心论点是健康成人买补剂It could just be a waste of money——讲\n的是补剂浪费钱，而陈述句Q44的核心是“天然食物是最好的蛋白来源”——立场对象\n不同。\nIt could just be a waste of money\nB B段陈述的是protein is present in all foods这一分布事实，没有“天然食物是最佳来\n源”这种价值判断；陈述句best source强调的是“最佳”这一立场表态，必须落在D段。\nprotein is present in all foods", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "People are advised to drink more liquid when they take in more protein.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】锚定“多吃蛋白+多喝水”\n【定位】Pipitone says if you increase protein, you also have to increase your fluid intake.\nPipitone说如果你增加蛋白质摄入，你也得增加液体摄入量。\n【改写】drink more liquid ↔ increase your fluid intake\n动作短语同义改写\nwhen they take in more protein ↔ if you increase protein\n时间状语对应条件状语\nPeople are advised to ↔ you also have to\n建议↔要求的等价\n【辨邻】 M M段焦点是肾脏负担和肾结石风险，与陈述句Q45“多喝水”的建议没有对应。\nextra protein makes the kidneys work harder\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 用蛋白粉/蛋白棒等蛋白质补剂的市场已成行业风口——With more than $12 billion in\nsales this year, the industry is booming，并预计到2025年还要卖出几十亿，但我们真\n的需要这么多额外的蛋白质吗？该段以这个总起问题引出全篇。\n信息指纹 120亿美元产业与2025预测\nB 蛋白质对身体每个细胞都关键，且和只在少数食物里出现的营养素不同——protein is\npresent in all foods，注册营养师Pipitone指出，The typical American diet is a lot\nhigher in protein than a lot of us think，蔬菜、水果、豆类、谷物里都有。\n信息指纹 蛋白质几乎无处不在\nC\n美国政府推荐成人每天蛋白质摄入量是50 to 60 grams of protein a day；段里用早餐\n两个鸡蛋加奶酪和橙子已经22 grams of protein、再加上午餐就over the\nrecommended 50 grams来说明，普通饮食轻松超标。\n信息指纹 RDA数字与早餐凑数算法\nD\n既然食物里就够吃，为什么还要补？Pipitone的回答是不用——Whole foods are\nalways the best option rather than adding supplements；并指出FDA对补剂的监管不\n像对食物和药物那么严，可能蛋白含量更少、糖更多甚至有咖啡因。\n信息指纹 天然食物优于补剂\nE 如果真要考虑买补剂，要看配料表；Pipitone说I've seen very expensive protein\nsupplements that claim to be high quality but they might not really be beneficial\nfor the average healthy adult，对一般健康成年人来说It could just be a waste\nof money。\n信息指纹 对健康成人是浪费钱\nF\n确实有些情况需要额外补——Anytime you're repairing or building muscle，比如极限\n耐力运动员、马拉松训练者、健美选手；蛋白质能speed along that process，帮他们\n在分解修复肌肉时占得先机。\n信息指纹 极限运动员加速修复\nG 纯素食者、忙得没空吃饭的人、术后/伤后恢复的人、以及老年人都能从额外蛋白质里\n获益；老年研究者Starr指出At around age 60, “muscles really start to break down”，\n因此the protein needs of an older adult actually increase。\n信息指纹 60岁后蛋白需求增加\nH\nStarr与同事Bales做过一项小研究，给obese older individuals who were trying to lose\nweight在饮食里加蛋白食物，六个月后the high protein group had significantly\nimproved their muscle function，几乎是对照组的两倍。\n信息指纹 肥胖减重老人加蛋白实验\nI Starr下一步在研究高蛋白饮食是否也改善老年人肌肉本身的质量，她用CT扫描测量肌\n肉大小和脂肪，将高蛋白饮食的老年人与常规饮食的老年人作对比；段末说her\nfindings should be available in a couple of months。\n信息指纹 CT扫描后续研究\nJ 70岁的Corliss Keith是Starr上一项研究里高蛋白组的受试者，她现身说法称I feel like I\nhave a different body, I have more energy, I'm stronger，能一周上三次Zumba课、跑\n步机锻炼、长距离快走，还减重15磅以上。\n信息指纹 Keith现身说法\nK Starr总结说人随年龄增长，肌肉力量是让他们继续在自己家里独立生活的关键；Keith\n本人也回应说I feel like I could stay by myself until I'm 100。\n信息指纹 肌力支撑独立居家\nL 蛋白质会不会摄入过量？Pipitone说要小心：吃太多会导致痉挛、头痛、疲劳，还有\nDehydration的风险；她强调如果增加蛋白，必须同时增加饮水量——if you increase\nprotein, you also have to increase your fluid intake。\n信息指纹 增蛋白必须同步增水\nM\n有迹象表明，额外蛋白质会让the kidneys work harder，对有肾病史的人可能成问\n题，补剂可能increase the risk of kidney stones。\n信息指纹 增加肾脏负担与肾结石风险\nN 底线建议：如果你觉得自己需要更多蛋白，先问自己是不是极限运动员、是不是术后/\n伤后恢复、是不是60岁以上；如果是，加鸡蛋肉类等高蛋白食物有益；不确定就咨询\n初级保健医生。\n信息指纹 三类人群清单与就医建议", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about attitude?", "options": ["A) It shapes our beliefs and ideologies.", "B) It improves our psychological wellbeing.", "C) It determines how we respond to our immediate environment.", "D) It changes the way we think, feel and interact with one another."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about attitude 问的是 attitude 这一概念的本质特\n征，需要在原文找一句对 attitude 功能的定义句去对答案，本质仍是细节定位，而非全篇\n主旨。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about attitude 是疑问句结构：主语 we、谓语\nlearn、from the passage 表来源、about attitude 表对象，无倒装无否定，直白提问对\nattitude 的认识。\n【定位】We use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us.\n我们用我们的态度去过滤、解读并对周围的世界作出反应。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt shapes our beliefs and ideologies. 把 attitude 说成“塑造我们的信念和意识形态”，\n但原文是 attitudes…towards particular objects, people, events or ideologies，\nideologies 是 attitude 的对象，不是 attitude 塑造的产物。\nB) 无中生有\nIt improves our psychological wellbeing. 中的 psychological wellbeing 在 P2 没有\n对应表述，原文只说 attitude 是 one of our most powerful psychological tools，强\n调“工具”不是“增进健康”。\nC) ✓ 正确\nreact to the world around us ↔ respond to our immediate environment\nWe use our attitudes to filter, interpret and react ↔ It determines how we respond\nIt determines how we respond to our immediate environment 与定位句 We use\nour attitudes to filter, interpret and react to the world around us. 在“施动者—动作\n—对象”三层均一一对应，是同义改写。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt changes the way we think, feel and interact with one another. 把原文 react to\nthe world around us 改写成“与彼此互动”，并加了 changes（改变）这一动词，与原\n文“使用 attitudes 去 react”不一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What can contribute to the refinement of one's attitude, according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Their idols' behaviors.", "B) Their educational level.", "C) Their contact with the opposite gender.", "D) Their interaction with different cultures."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What can contribute to the refinement of one's attitude 问的是“致使”项，是对原文列\n举式因果句的细节查找。\n【拆句】What can contribute to the refinement of one's attitude, according to the passage\n主语 What、谓语 can contribute to、宾语 refinement of one's attitude；according to\nthe passage 是范围状语；句首特殊疑问句无倒装陷阱。\n【定位】As you acquire a distinctive identity your attitudes are further refined by the behavior of those\nwith whom you identify—your family, those of your gender and culture, and the people you\nadmire, even though you may not know them personally.\n当你逐渐获得独立的身份认同，你的态度会被你所认同的那些人——你的家人、与你同性别\n同文化的人、以及你所敬仰的人（即便你并不与他们相识）——的行为进一步精炼。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe people you admire ↔ Their idols' behaviors.\n破折号列举中明确含 the people you admire，Their idols' behaviors. 正是它的名词\n化同义改写，符合预判。\nB) 无中生有\nTheir educational level. 中的 educational level 在 P3 没有任何对应表述，全段只谈身\n份、性别文化、敬仰者和信息重复。\nC) 与原文相反\nTheir contact with the opposite gender. 与原文 those of your gender and culture\n方向相反——原文强调的是与“同性别同文化”的认同者，C 却换成 opposite gender。\nD) 与原文相反\nTheir interaction with different cultures. 与原文 those of your gender and culture\n也方向相反——原文是“同文化者”，D 是“与不同文化互动”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do many studies find about people's feelings and thoughts?", "options": ["A) They may not suggest how a person is going to behave.", "B) They are in a way consistent with a person's mentality.", "C) They may not find expression in interpersonal relations.", "D) They are in line with a person's behavior no matter what."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do many studies find 是查找原文对“many studies 的发现”这一明确事实的陈述。\n【拆句】What do many studies find about people's feelings and thoughts 标准 wh-疑问句：\n助动词 do 提到主语前，主语 many studies、谓语 find、about 短语作宾语补足；结构平\n直无陷阱。\n【定位】However, many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't necessarily\npredict behavior.\n然而，许多研究发现，感受和想法未必能预示行为。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndon't necessarily predict behavior ↔ may not suggest how a person is going to behave\nThey may not suggest how a person is going to behave. 以 They=feelings and\nthoughts 为主语，把 don't necessarily predict behavior 一字不漏地等值改写，符合\n预判。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey are in a way consistent with a person's mentality. 说感受/想法与“心智”在某种\n程度上一致，但原文 many studies have found that feelings and thoughts don't\nnecessarily predict behavior 强调的是与“行为”未必一致，B 既偷换对象又调转方向。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey may not find expression in interpersonal relations. 把原文 behavior 偷换为\ninterpersonal relations（人际关系），属于话题外延的偷换。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey are in line with a person's behavior no matter what. 用 no matter what 说“无\n论如何都一致”，与原文 don't necessarily predict behavior 完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How come many people don't do what they believe is good?", "options": ["A) They can't afford the time.", "B) They have no idea how to.", "C) They are hypocritical.", "D) They lack willpower."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How come many people don't do what they believe is good 是“why”的口语化变体，\n仍是对原文因果句的细节查找。\n【拆句】How come many people don't do what they believe is good 中 How come 后接陈述\n语序的从句 many people don't do what they believe is good；what 引导宾语从句作\ndo 的宾语，结构虽嵌套但语义直接。\n【定位】That's why, for example, many say they believe in the benefits of recycling or exercise, but\ndon't behave in line with their views, because it takes awareness, effort and courage to go\nbeyond merely stating that you believe something is a good idea.\n这就是为什么，比如说，很多人嘴上说他们相信回收或运动的好处，却没有按自己的想法去\n做——因为要超越仅仅“嘴上说自己相信某事是好主意”这一步，需要觉察、努力和勇气。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey can't afford the time. 把原因归为没时间，但 because it takes awareness,\neffort and courage 三个名词中均不含时间因素。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey have no idea how to. 把原因归为“不知道怎么做”，但原文 because it takes\nawareness, effort and courage 强调的是意志品质，而不是方法/知识。\nC) 过度推断\nThey are hypocritical. 把“嘴上说相信但不做”推断为伪善，但原文只说需要\nawareness/effort/courage 才能落实，并未对人格作伪善评价。\nD) ✓ 正确\nit takes awareness, effort and courage to go beyond merely stating that you believe something is\na good idea\n↔ They lack willpower.\nThey lack willpower. 把原文 awareness, effort and courage 三种并列意志品质归并\n为上位词 willpower，并以 lack 表达“需要却没有”，是同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is proposed as a strategy to change attitude?", "options": ["A) Changing things that require one's immediate attention.", "B) Starting to act in a way that embodies one's aspirations.", "C) Adjusting one's behavior gradually over a period of time.", "D) Considering ways of reducing one's psychological burdens."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is proposed as a strategy to change attitude 问“被提出的策略是什么”，是对末段\n明示策略句的细节查找。\n【拆句】What is proposed as a strategy to change attitude 是被动语态特殊疑问句：What 作主\n语、is proposed 是被动谓语、as a strategy to change attitude 是方式状语；考生要意\n识到“被提出的东西＝作者推荐的策略”。\n【定位】One of the most effective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel\nand think the way you'd prefer to.\n改变态度最有效的方法之一，是从现在开始就按你希望自己已经持有的感受和想法去行动。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nChanging things that require one's immediate attention. 把策略说成“去改变那些需\n要立即关注的事”，但 P5 没有 immediate attention 这一概念，反而强调按理想状态\n行动。\nB) ✓ 正确\nstart behaving as if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to ↔\nStarting to act in a way that embodies one's aspirations.\nas if you already feel and think the way you'd prefer to ↔\nin a way that embodies one's aspirations\nStarting to act in a way that embodies one's aspirations. 与 One of the most\neffective ways to change an attitude is to start behaving as if you already feel\nand think the way you'd prefer to. 在动作（start behaving=Starting to act）、方\n式（as if…the way you'd prefer to=in a way that embodies one's aspirations）两\n层逐对对应，是同义改写。\nC) 与原文相反\nAdjusting one's behavior gradually over a period of time. 用 gradually over a\nperiod of time 表“逐步”，但原文 start behaving—right now 强调“现在就开始”，方\n向与节奏均相反。\nD) 偷换概念\nConsidering ways of reducing one's psychological burdens. 把策略说成“减轻心理\n负担”，但 Is there anything you consider a burden rather than a privilege? If so,\nstart behaving—right now—as if the latter is the case. 不是要“减轻 burden”，而是\n要按 privilege 的视角去行动。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does Greenpeace's study find about krill fishing?", "options": ["A) It caused a great many penguins and whales to migrate.", "B) It was depriving penguins and whales of their habitats.", "C) It was carried out too close to the habitats of penguins and whales.", "D) It posed an unprecedented threat to the wildlife around Antarctica."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Greenpeace's study find about krill fishing? 直接问\"研究发现什么\"，指向原\n文中可直接抽取的事实陈述。\n【拆句】What does Greenpeace's study find about krill fishing? 是标准 wh-疑问句，主语\nGreenpeace's study、谓语 find、介词短语 about krill fishing 限定范围；结构平铺直叙，\n无陷阱。\n【定位】The study by Greenpeace analysed the movements of krill fishing vessels in the region and\nfound they were increasingly operating \"in the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and\nwhale-feeding grounds\".\nGreenpeace 的研究分析了该地区磷虾船的航行轨迹，发现它们越来越多地在\"紧邻企鹅栖\n息地和鲸鱼觅食区\"的水域作业。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\ncaused a great many penguins and whales to migrate 中\"造成大量企鹅鲸鱼迁徙\"在\n原文找不到对应——P2 只说船在栖息地附近作业，未说动物迁徙。\nB) 过度推断\ndepriving penguins and whales of their habitats 中 deprive...of 表\"剥夺\"，比原\n文\"在附近作业\"严重得多；原文未说磷虾产业\"夺走\"栖息地。\nC) ✓ 正确\nin the immediate vicinity of penguin colonies and whale-feeding grounds ↔\ntoo close to the habitats of penguins and whales\nthey were increasingly operating ↔ It was carried out\nC 把\"紧邻栖息地\"重写为\"太靠近栖息地\"，与预判完全吻合，是定位句的客观转述。\nD) 范围程度偷改\nposed an unprecedented threat 中 unprecedented（前所未有的）是程度副词性夸\n张；原文 posed a serious threat 只说\"严重威胁\"，未说\"前所未有\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "For what purpose has a global campaign been launched?", "options": ["A) To reduce the impact of climate change on Antarctica.", "B) To establish conservation areas in the Antarctic region.", "C) To regulate krill fishing operations in the Antarctic seas.", "D) To publicise the concern about the impact of krill fishing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】For what purpose has a global campaign been launched? 问的是已发生事件的具体目\n的，对应原文中一句可抽取的陈述。\n【拆句】For what purpose has a global campaign been launched? 是现在完成时被动语态疑问\n句，by 主语被省略；只需聚焦目的状语\"For what purpose\"，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】A global campaign has been launched to create a network of ocean sanctuaries to protect the\nseas in the region and Greenpeace is calling for an immediate halt to fishing in areas being\nconsidered for sanctuary status.\n已发起一项全球运动，旨在建立一个海洋保护区网络以保护该地区的海域；Greenpeace 呼\n吁立即停止在被考虑列为保护区的海域进行捕捞。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTo reduce the impact of climate change on Antarctica 把原文 growing concern\nabout the impact of fishing and climate change 中\"气候变化\"这一背景诱因当成运\n动目的——但运动目的是建保护区，不是直接降低气候变化影响。\nB) ✓ 正确\nto create a network of ocean sanctuaries ↔\nTo establish conservation areas in the Antarctic region\nthe seas in the region ↔ in the Antarctic region\nB 把\"建立海洋保护区网络\"压缩为\"建立南极地区的保护区\"，与预判完全吻合。\nC) 以偏概全\nTo regulate krill fishing operations 中 regulate（监管/规范）的力度远不及原文 an\nimmediate halt to fishing（立即停止）；且原文是 all fishing，不仅磷虾。\nD) 偷换概念\nTo publicise the concern about the impact of krill fishing 把原文 amid growing\nconcern 这一背景描述误读成运动目的——运动是因这种担忧而生，不是为传播担忧\n而生。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is Greenpeace's recommendation to the krill industry?", "options": ["A) Opting to operate away from the suggested conservation areas.", "B) Volunteering to protect the endangered species in the Antarctic.", "C) Refraining from krill fishing throughout the breeding season.", "D) Showing its sense of responsibility by leading the global campaign."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is Greenpeace's recommendation to the krill industry? 问的是建议\"是什么\"，原\n文有 Greenpeace 发言人的直接引语可抽取。\n【拆句】What is Greenpeace's recommendation to the krill industry? 是简单 wh-疑问句，介词\n短语 to the krill industry 限定建议方向；无陷阱。\n【定位】If the krill industry wants to show it's a responsible player, then it should be voluntarily\ngetting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary, and should instead be\nbacking the protection of these huge tracts of the Antarctic.\n如果磷虾产业想表明自己是负责任的一方，那么它就应该自愿撤出任何被提议为海洋保护区\n的海域，转而支持对这些南极广阔海域的保护。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nvoluntarily getting out of any area which is being proposed as an ocean sanctuary ↔\nOpting to operate away from the suggested conservation areas\nan ocean sanctuary ↔ the suggested conservation areas\nA 把\"自愿撤出拟议保护区\"重写为\"选择远离拟议保护区作业\"，与预判完全吻合。\nB) 偷换概念\nVolunteering to protect the endangered species in the Antarctic 中\"保护濒危物\n种\"在原文找不到——原文建议是\"撤出\"，不是\"保护物种\"；而且\"endangered\nspecies\"也未出现在原文。\nC) 张冠李戴\nRefraining from krill fishing throughout the breeding season\"在整个繁殖季节禁\n渔\"在 P4 找不到对应；\"季节限制\"是 P7 CCAMLR 的说法，与 Greenpeace 的建议张冠\n李戴。\nD) 过度推断\nShowing its sense of responsibility by leading the global campaign 把原文 wants\nto show it's a responsible player 这一假设条件夸大为\"通过领导全球运动\"——原文从\n未让磷虾产业\"主导\"运动。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What did CCAMLR aim to do according to its science manager?", "options": ["A) Raise public awareness of the vulnerability of Antarctic species.", "B) Ban all commercial fishing operations in the Southern Ocean.", "C) Keep the penguin colonies from all fishing interference.", "D) Sustain fishing without damaging the Antarctic ecosystem."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did CCAMLR aim to do according to its science manager? 问 CCAMLR 的目标，\n且明确言论来源，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】What did CCAMLR aim to do according to its science manager? 是带宾语从句感的\nwh-疑问句；aim to do 表目标，according to 限定信息来源，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Keith Reid, a science manager at CCAMLR, said that the organisation sought \"a balance\nbetween protection, conservation and sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean.\"\nCCAMLR 的科学经理 Keith Reid 说，该组织寻求\"在南大洋的保护、养护与可持续捕捞之间\n的平衡\"。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nRaise public awareness of the vulnerability of Antarctic species 中\"提升公众对南极\n物种脆弱性的认识\"在原文找不到——CCAMLR 的科学经理未提\"科普\"目标。\nB) 与原文相反\nBan all commercial fishing operations in the Southern Ocean 主张\"禁止所有商业捕\n捞\"，与 sustainable fishing in the Southern Ocean（可持续捕捞）正话反说——\nCCAMLR 是支持可持续捕捞，不是全面禁渔。\nC) 过度推断\nKeep the penguin colonies from all fishing interference 中\"避免所有捕捞干扰企\n鹅\"在原文是季节性事实 more fishing was taking place nearer penguin colonies it\nwas often happening later in the season when these colonies were empty，而非\nCCAMLR 的目标；且\"all fishing interference\"是绝对化夸大。\nD) ✓ 正确\na balance between protection, conservation and sustainable fishing ↔\nSustain fishing without damaging the Antarctic ecosystem\nprotection, conservation ↔ without damaging the Antarctic ecosystem\nsustainable fishing ↔ Sustain fishing\nD 把\"保护-养护-可持续捕捞之间的平衡\"重写为\"在不破坏生态前提下维持捕捞\"，与预判\n完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How does CCAMLR define its role in the conservation of the Antarctic environment?", "options": ["A) A coordinator in policy discussions.", "B) An authority on big data analysis.", "C) A provider of the needed expertise.", "D) An initiator of marine sanctuaries."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】How does CCAMLR define its role in the conservation of the Antarctic environment?\n问\"如何定义\"，回到原文 CCAMLR 的自我表态句即可抽取。\n【拆句】How does CCAMLR define its role in the conservation of the Antarctic environment?\n是 how-疑问句，主语 CCAMLR、谓语 define、宾语 its role，介词短语 in the\nconservation... 限定角色场景；无陷阱。\n【定位】We are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas, but we hope to\ncontribute positively with our knowledge and experience.\n我们不是决定设立海洋保护区的人，但我们希望以我们的知识与经验作出正面贡献。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nA coordinator in policy discussions 中\"政策讨论中的协调者\"是无中生有——原文只\n说 CCAMLR 与 NGO 有 open dialogue 与 talks，未自我定位为\"协调者\"。\nB) 偷换概念\nAn authority on big data analysis 中\"大数据分析权威\"在原文找不到——原文只提\nlatest scientific data，没说 CCAMLR 是大数据权威。\nC) ✓ 正确\ncontribute positively with our knowledge and experience ↔\nA provider of the needed expertise\nWe are not the ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas ↔\nA provider of the needed expertise\nC 用\"提供专业知识者\"准确浓缩了 but 后的自我表态，且与 but 前的\"非决策者\"立场一\n致——完美匹配预判。\nD) 与原文相反\nAn initiator of marine sanctuaries 中\"海洋保护区的发起者\"直接与原文 We are not\nthe ones to decide on the establishment of marine protected areas 相悖——\nCCAMLR 自陈不是决策者，更不是发起者。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
